window.DLI_SCRIPT=[{"id":"cover","index":0,"title":"SO-101 Sim-to-Real","steps":[{"id":"cover:cue:0","title":"The workflow","html":"<p>Today we will follow the full robot-learning workflow through one task: placing vials in a rack. We will collect demonstrations, adapt GR00T, and evaluate in simulation and on the physical SO-101. Failures will guide the next data collection and experiment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"cover","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"index.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"cc906d3c7fff1805427112be0d1cb6fed5af5941874387ce2bc3c784696fa514"}},{"id":"cover:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Let&#x27;s start with today&#x27;s task and see what we will learn through this workflow.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"cover","kind":"slide","index":1}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-slide","page":"index.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"fb291101148bff6fc34d687e2d86b1cec78de714a80f95c6851cb7ded8b2bb6b"}}]},{"id":"topic--index--1","index":1,"title":"Train an SO-101 Robot From Sim-to-Real With NVIDIA Isaac","steps":[{"id":"topic--index--1:cue:0","title":"What connects the workflow","html":"<p>Our goal is to transfer behavior learned in simulation to the physical SO-101. Isaac Lab defines the task, Newton computes the physics, and ovrtx renders camera images. LeRobot connects collection, training, and evaluation; GR00T predicts actions from observations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--index--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"index.html","anchor":"overview","source_sha256":"f6dc79c1a87d1fb05ad27bdfb4205c9c26fc6b69a70e3c7b1f70cd3ef1ec576f"}},{"id":"topic--index--1:cue:7","title":"The exercise sequence","html":"<p>We first align the arms and cameras, then collect simulation demonstrations with varied conditions. Real evaluation will guide additions of real data, corrections, video augmentation, and physics calibration. Prepared checkpoints let us compare behavior without waiting for a complete training run.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Image 1 · Robot Calibration","action":{"slide":"topic--index--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"overview","text":"Robot Calibration"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--index--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--index--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"overview","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"index.html","anchor":"overview","source_sha256":"920de4dfc46a54427f91f48a31d275399dea57cbaf1fa64ea92f303641ed4ed5"}},{"id":"topic--index--1:cue:12","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Let us see how this workflow fits into the day.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--index--1","kind":"slide","index":2}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"index.html","anchor":"train-an-so-101-robot-from-sim-to-real-with-nvidia-isaac","source_sha256":"b48096d7ec8aa830bce880218cd903e9a33404fb1a3252403842478ed839ceec"}}]},{"id":"agenda","index":2,"title":"Today’s Schedule","steps":[{"id":"agenda:cue:0","title":"The day ahead","html":"<p>In the morning, calibration and teleoperation establish consistent inputs, followed by GR00T training concepts and simulation evaluation. In the afternoon, real rollouts reveal failures that we address with Co-Training, HG-DAgger, Cosmos, and System Identification.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"agenda","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"bf47d49b4966e5dd8282ddefd11ef3778ec3ff4abc32c4bca19fde7f0fedd538"}},{"id":"agenda:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We will learn each concept in a lecture and apply it to the same task in the hands-on exercises. To establish the foundation for this workflow, let’s begin with Physical AI and Robot Learning: how a robot observes, acts, and uses the result as its next input.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"agenda","kind":"slide","index":3}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"602be04592d0d45a0eff4fc91d3951bcba26632031b3ac9692eb014f8bf20433"}}]},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1","index":3,"title":"Physical AI and Robot Learning","steps":[{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:cue:0","title":"Observe and act","html":"<p>Physical AI observes the physical world through sensors and changes it through goal-directed actions. The result becomes the next observation, so the observation–control loop matters beyond any single prediction.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"what-is-physical-ai","source_sha256":"bfd409b94dd5fa2ab79d593d89349dc5a51b07bc7225866f5a67bd14389f5969"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:cue:9","title":"From goal to action","html":"<p>In this loop, language specifies the goal of placing a vial in the rack. GR00T receives wrist and external images plus current joint positions and predicts an action sequence. After execution, new images and joint feedback inform the next decision.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Describe the task","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":0,"media":1,"kind":"diagram","term":"instruction"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"what-is-physical-ai","source_sha256":"3bc1e23eefb90c6413a474a6cf56f0b68f41d97e6868a5af6accc5aed2c1fada"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:diagram:0:1:observe","title":"Observe the current scene","html":"<p>The policy receives camera images and the robot’s joint positions. New observations help it respond as the scene changes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"observe"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/physical-ai-loop.html","term":"observe"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:diagram:0:1:predict","title":"Predict actions","html":"<p>A trained policy maps observations and the task instruction to an action sequence. Inference uses the learned weights; it does not train the model during each move.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"predict"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/physical-ai-loop.html","term":"predict"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:diagram:0:1:act","title":"Act, then observe again","html":"<p>Motors carry out the commanded motion. What the robot actually does becomes part of its next observation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"act"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/physical-ai-loop.html","term":"act"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:cue:12","title":"Task breadth and proficiency","html":"<p>The range of tasks handled by this loop distinguishes specialists from generalists. Specialists focus on a narrow task; generalists aim to handle multiple tasks and conditions. Today, post-training adapts the broadly trained GR00T model to the SO-101 vial task.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"specialist-and-generalist-robots","text":"A specialist learns a narrow task. A generalist aims to handle a wider range of tasks; post-training adapts that broad f","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"specialist-and-generalist-robots","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"specialist-and-generalist-robots","source_sha256":"875c6cbc0f6733680ad3176522c87e07703808c409f52e5cc06fbbe379a7cc51"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:cue:14","title":"Connecting the robot examples","html":"<p>Applied to these examples, a production line is closer to a specialist repeating a defined task. Diverse manipulation and humanoid examples illustrate ambitions for broader task coverage. Robot appearance alone does not establish generality; the tasks and conditions it can actually handle do.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 2","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":1,"media":1,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":1,"media":1,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","pane":1,"media":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"specialist-and-generalist-robots","source_sha256":"a6b97b3f13340b446aba35f91292c8ccbfacd87f5cb11bc7856707017ede30b6"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--1:cue:15","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Let&#x27;s take a closer look at the vial pick-and-place task we will teach GR00T.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--1","kind":"slide","index":4}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"what-is-physical-ai","source_sha256":"ba7c5b39c28605bcfc2e1e6e28180f6403b3ce64af9676a5f3e847e5aed80e45"}}]},{"id":"topic--01-overview--2","index":4,"title":"Today's Task · Vial Pick-and-Place","steps":[{"id":"topic--01-overview--2:cue:0","title":"The task and comparison setting","html":"<p>The task is to pick up scattered vials and place them in a designated rack. We learn this behavior in simulation and transfer it to the real robot. A lightbox and bounded workspace control some surrounding conditions so we can study differences between the two environments.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"the-task-centrifuge-vial-pick-and-place","source_sha256":"698e0196557ecd78265d4622aba5299832f3ac100eba55a2e0a6aad1139a43f5"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--2:cue:5","title":"Motion and success","html":"<p>The motion proceeds through approach, grasp, transport, and placement. Grasping alone is insufficient: the vial must be placed stably in the rack. Recording the failure stage tells us which demonstrations to collect next.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 1","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"the-task-centrifuge-vial-pick-and-place","source_sha256":"e72e61d2072cf84a14d84f1ef7d2ecf7cb2a718e74c7d6af5ce79fde66df9088"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--2:cue:7","title":"Precision from two images","html":"<p>The policy uses two RGB views without depth to determine vial orientation and rack location. After grasping, wrist-view occlusion makes the external view more important. Teleoperating from those same views will make these input limitations concrete.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--2","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"why-is-this-problem-interesting","text":"Our policy will work from 2D camera information, and the placement of the vials in the rack requires re-orienting the vi","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"why-is-this-problem-interesting","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"why-is-this-problem-interesting","source_sha256":"69a772b77b0ea2cd95407c543b972312a638e4969e8a24875a45af44aa532507"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--2:cue:10","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>How do we teach a robot a task that involves occlusion and precise placement? Demonstrations of the task provide the starting point for learning.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--2","kind":"slide","index":5}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"the-task-centrifuge-vial-pick-and-place","source_sha256":"b79dec74a9d3a05326c8318a21b50da08f6a38d61805f894c6a44b21a8d73cc4"}}]},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3","index":5,"title":"Learning from Demonstrations and Expanding the Data","steps":[{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:cue:0","title":"What demonstrations teach","html":"<p>A demonstration aligns the current camera images and robot state with the action supplied by a person. Imitation learning learns this relationship to select actions for new observations. Video alone does not identify the robot commands that produced the motion.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"learning-from-demonstrations","source_sha256":"42e258bd43dfb28f2decaf7dc144e2f5f9481ff2bdeebefaba2f5a88c34d6b74"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:cue:4","title":"From collection to the next experiment","html":"<p>We collect episodes through teleoperation or a scripted controller, then inspect view–action alignment, starting conditions, and success or failure segments. After adapting the pretrained policy, new trials expose the situations missing from our next collection.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 1 · Collect demonstrations","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"demonstrate"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"learning-from-demonstrations","source_sha256":"749bf463d2cc271c9e3ef455d9e231d74dec03bb05a22af5b79886c1986b7eca"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:diagram:0:0:review","title":"2 · Inspect the examples","html":"<p>Review episodes before training. Check the task, views, starting conditions and actions, including what happens after a grasp or placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"review"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/learning-from-demonstrations.html","term":"review"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:diagram:0:0:train","title":"3 · Adapt a pretrained policy","html":"<p>Post-training adjusts a pretrained model using demonstrations from the robot and task. It learns an observation-to-action mapping rather than replaying one recorded path.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"train"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/learning-from-demonstrations.html","term":"train"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:diagram:0:0:evaluate","title":"4 · Test and iterate","html":"<p>Evaluate the learned policy on fresh trials. Simulation and hardware answer different questions, so keep their outcomes separate and use failures to plan the next experiment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"evaluate"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/learning-from-demonstrations.html","term":"evaluate"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:cue:10","title":"Today’s training stage","html":"<p>Capability can be scaled through pre-training, post-training, and inference. Today we build on broad pre-training and focus on post-training for the SO-101 observation and action interface.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"scaling-physical-ai","text":"Broad pre-training, task-specific post-training, and additional computation at inference are different ways to develop c","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"scaling-physical-ai","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"scaling-physical-ai","source_sha256":"f584426147c9a01373b8d473fc82332ecf615d9f291754ce23847ae666548ddd"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:cue:14","title":"The cost of robot data","html":"<p>Robot data needs to connect the observed state, the action issued, and the resulting change. Learning requires aligned image and joint timestamps as well as consistent units and robot calibration. Collection cost includes this recording and inspection work in addition to performing the motion.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 1","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"why-robot-data-takes-work","source_sha256":"f5997e880e8db14a27323051987ff1935415d04bb47070d7a942ab6fcf65f75d"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--3:cue:15","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Collecting enough demonstrations takes time and requires varied conditions. Next, we will see how simulation helps with that collection effort.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--3","kind":"slide","index":6}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"learning-from-demonstrations","source_sha256":"7efefdaec2e1db3e6dbd6a6def888f4c12bd7a090c14fc4940f5ca4521fc6060"}}]},{"id":"topic--01-overview--4","index":6,"title":"Why Use Simulation?","steps":[{"id":"topic--01-overview--4:cue:0","title":"Repeat experiments in simulation","html":"<p>Simulation reduces this collection burden. Resets repeat trials with less hardware wear or collision risk, while software varies object placement and lighting. Parallel execution is possible, though actual throughput depends on the scene and hardware.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--4","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"why-simulation-matters","source_sha256":"5d55a0754b1a83bdf3fd7a31b94e2f481902df911a0de0b5b47f46893304f896"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--4:cue:7","title":"Exact state for data generation","html":"<p>Simulation exposes exact object poses, contacts, and segmentation. These can support scripted controllers and episode checks, while the deployed policy uses camera and joint-state inputs available on the physical robot.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--4","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"privileged-information","text":"Simulation also provides access to information that might be impossible to obtain in the real world:","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"privileged-information","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"privileged-information","source_sha256":"11101eea2c9839192a9caea334f288f101744546edc6580c206be1af8b4c9d30"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--4:cue:10","title":"What remains to test on hardware","html":"<p>Simulation expands experimentation but does not eliminate differences in cameras, contact, or actuators. We train with varied simulation data, then use real rollouts to identify the real data or model calibration still needed.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--4","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"key-takeaways","text":"Simulation enables fast, safe, diverse training that can be impossible in the real world","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"key-takeaways","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"key-takeaways","source_sha256":"7052da3e013f549249c217af18fe2597f61e051b928edcf82216563778d6d2c6"}},{"id":"topic--01-overview--4:cue:12","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>During the hands-on work, we will use Codex and the course Skills to find relevant explanations and commands.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--01-overview--4","kind":"slide","index":7}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"01-overview.html","anchor":"why-simulation-matters","source_sha256":"b281483abbe35a6b0861913af359e8291ba8c3b94038d77774980641b6f5b57b"}}]},{"id":"topic--01-codex--1","index":7,"title":"CODEX · Connect the Course Folder and Skills","steps":[{"id":"topic--01-codex--1:cue:0","title":"Connect the same checkout","html":"<p>Connect Codex App or CLI to the course repository. Use the same local checkout mounted into the container and the aidayseoul2026 branch so explanations match the executed code. In the app, select This computer / Local.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--1","pane":-1,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"codex","source_sha256":"ccfda84778d0882ceced478ad699c8833d85d349c7d056cc83bc89e1260113fc"}},{"id":"topic--01-codex--1:cue:5","title":"Verify the guide and skill","html":"<p>Ask it to read the current path and branch, AGENTS.md, and skills/so101-dli/SKILL.md. Confirm that the reported path matches the course folder so questions refer to the same materials and execution environment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 4 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--1","pane":0,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--1","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"option-a-chatgpt-app","source_sha256":"b40b384d841831a978ff369c74fa8599426e7b2fa9f889d390d788a55d342dca"}},{"id":"topic--01-codex--1:cue:8","title":"CLI and source references","html":"<p>Run the CLI from the course folder on the host as well. Asking which guide section and code support an answer makes it easier to connect the explanation to the exercise.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 4 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"option-b-codex-cli","source_sha256":"c780240a0e8b2a4a5d8b8dc4b70dc32b959d5334b08f1c7ff938ec83964c21da"}},{"id":"topic--01-codex--1:cue:14","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>With the course folder and Skills connected, we can try examples of course questions and hands-on requests.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--1","kind":"slide","index":8}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"option-a-chatgpt-app","source_sha256":"2cb98a6fd2a4f70682b6362e72cc24fa4be930d795047805560a50b6617c795b"}}]},{"id":"topic--01-codex--2","index":8,"title":"CODEX · Ask Questions and Continue the Exercises","steps":[{"id":"topic--01-codex--2:cue:0","title":"Include the current state","html":"<p>Include your goal and current state in exercise questions. For setup, check the GPU, container, and device connections. For concepts, ask for an explanation in terms of today’s vial task.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"environment-setup","source_sha256":"4757adb25456182d23232a350dd22f4703d88c5e5183ba93e6a08ab2c27cf27b"}},{"id":"topic--01-codex--2:cue:9","title":"Check the document connection","html":"<p>First ask for today’s schedule to verify the document connection. It should match the course schedule in KST: a 09:00 start, lunch from 12:45 to 13:45, and a 17:15 finish.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--2","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--2","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"first-example-ask-for-todays-schedule","source_sha256":"760e873fcdd48b109327cbc97a958d907300d665b28c29a64d26440273a2319b"}},{"id":"topic--01-codex--2:cue:16","title":"Information for diagnosis","html":"<p>For errors, provide the command, whether it ran on the host or in the container, and the actual error message. For comparisons, include the checkpoint and evaluation conditions. Run motion commands only after preparing the devices and workspace.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--2","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"try-course-questions-and-hands-on-requests","text":"For an error, provide the command, whether it ran on the Host or in the Container, and the relevant error text. Codex ca","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"try-course-questions-and-hands-on-requests","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"try-course-questions-and-hands-on-requests","source_sha256":"ea8a4e6d736e96eade2717a520e88687796c08d9a5ae3bd997fe97131294054d"}},{"id":"topic--01-codex--2:cue:17","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Now let us look at the hardware and software used in the exercises, beginning with the roles of the SO-101 leader and follower.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--01-codex--2","kind":"slide","index":9}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"01-codex.html","anchor":"environment-setup","source_sha256":"04c72e9e887b22048fc86c68f44c595b363afa1e9faea0175b96bc83a6b175e9"}}]},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--1","index":9,"title":"SO-101 · Leader and Follower","steps":[{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--1:cue:0","title":"The two arm roles","html":"<p>The SO-101 leader is the hand-operated input device; the follower performs the task. During teleoperation, the leader supplies actions. During autonomous evaluation, the policy takes that role. The teleop and robot command options refer to these two roles.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"the-so-101-robot","source_sha256":"a654ab828861c43edd34ec1442bc74fd306e0afb37d3682c94876902df872d8a"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--1:cue:6","title":"Joints and the end effector","html":"<p>Joint rotations combine to determine the end-effector position and orientation. Calibration aligns each encoder reference and, together, the whole-arm posture interpreted by the policy.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show video 1","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Play","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"video","operation":"play"}},{"label":"Pause","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"video","operation":"pause"}},{"label":"Restart video","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"video","operation":"replay"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"the-so-101-robot","source_sha256":"7404838f61c67d8006f3d1373536dbda2af8d13f0e5db0f47f626db6277fdd24"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":0,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--1:cue:10","title":"Mechanical condition matters","html":"<p>SO-101 accessibility and LeRobot support make it suitable for learning the full workflow. Backlash or loose fasteners can change actual motion under the same command, so precise placement failures also require checking the mechanism.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"why-so-101","text":"What makes it difficult for precision tasks:","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"why-so-101","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"why-so-101","source_sha256":"cda9e219fd85e583fdd3e31a9a793bf00627eb3153140bd82deb6ca8488b5cdc"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--1:cue:11","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>With the roles of the two arms established, we can look at LeRobot, which supports data collection and policy execution.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--1","kind":"slide","index":10}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"the-so-101-robot","source_sha256":"93d447b660b368d2bc1abd6cbefa6ca5816091eeee95494eb917b586bbcb3a42"}}]},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--2","index":10,"title":"LeRobot · From Collection to Evaluation","steps":[{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--2:cue:0","title":"Why use LeRobot?","html":"<p>LeRobot stores teleoperation and automated simulation collection in a common dataset format and connects it to training and rollout tools. Shared formatting still requires consistent camera names, joint order, and units.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"the-lerobot-project","source_sha256":"00507b61902a9e90dae22390f137888c2efbb18d9ed2e157c6348413a43d35bc"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--2:cue:9","title":"From local storage to the Hub","html":"<p>Simulation recordings are stored under datasets using the repo_id path. Verify HF_USER and the authenticated account, upload under that namespace, and tag the revision used for training. Inspect local episodes first and check the tag’s revision whenever the data changes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 4 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--2","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--2","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","source_sha256":"99ab0471542dd0cfab2fd31986ab8c7af0e558fe028877ab93821d32e18e696b"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--2:cue:13","title":"Train on inspected data","html":"<p>Inspect episodes from lerobot_agent or vials_state_machine in the Visualizer, then post-train GR00T with lerobot-train. Keep the input dataset ID distinct from the model repository and output directory.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--2","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","text":"Collect demonstrations with lerobot_agent and the leader arm, or use vials_state_machine for automated simulation collec","accordion":{"key":"lerobot-workflow","item":"post-training-pipeline"}}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","source_sha256":"ddedde30289b3131c408fa2bc1f3c5aa5535fdb59acae748aa68fbcdd5ee0817"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--2:cue:16","title":"Run the learned policy","html":"<p>In simulation, the policy server performs inference and lerobot_eval runs the environment. On hardware, lerobot-rollout uses the calibrated follower and camera configuration. Both require matching the checkpoint and its expected inputs.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--2","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","text":"In the same container terminal, start python -m lerobot.async_inference.policy_server in the background, then run lerobo","accordion":{"key":"lerobot-workflow","item":"simulation-evaluation"}}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"why-lerobot-for-this-course","source_sha256":"fc208886f1c237c4b5c27e576ed387b40d9748a860670944a553adb298b29711"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--2:cue:20","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Hugging Face Hub is where datasets and checkpoints are shared in this workflow. Next, we will open an episode and inspect its contents.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--2","kind":"slide","index":11}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"the-lerobot-project","source_sha256":"b93876133db3b8ec371fdd77dc5b53d5c489f18e42c885ea5caec91b59bea7ea"}}]},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--3","index":11,"title":"Hugging Face Hub and Dataset Visualizer","steps":[{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--3:cue:0","title":"Open a public Hub dataset","html":"<p>Let me demonstrate how to inspect public data shared through LeRobot and the Hub. Open FineART, or enter lerobot/FineART and select GO. It uses a different robot from SO-101, so first check its camera configuration and joint dimensions.</p><p><a href=\"https://huggingface.co/spaces/lerobot/visualize_dataset?path=/lerobot/FineART/episode_0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open Dataset Visualizer ↗</a></p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--3","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"dataset-visualizer","source_sha256":"5ea6bca8558e6d7a5fb5d6f8f35c2102862a7ea359526b608aa111c7da72050b"},"instructor_action":{"kind":"web-demo","url":"https://huggingface.co/spaces/lerobot/visualize_dataset?path=/lerobot/FineART/episode_0"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--3:cue:3","title":"Read images and trajectories together","html":"<p>Scrub through the episode and compare camera views with state and action at the same instant. Check for image discontinuities or abrupt motion changes, and follow grasp and placement through completion. The goal is to assess whether the continuous recording is suitable for learning.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--3","pane":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"dataset-visualizer","text":"Use this tool to explore any LeRobot dataset on the Hub. You can scrub through episodes, view camera feeds, and inspect ","accordion":null}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"dataset-visualizer","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"dataset-visualizer","source_sha256":"cb979a09d9c911d3a65a9ba041cdec1174c3d185bf0443651a58edcfe1d1df36"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--3:cue:15","title":"Sharing and reproducibility","html":"<p>When training on multiple datasets, check both schema compatibility and how often each source is sampled. Record the dataset revisions, training settings, checkpoint, and evaluation conditions together to reproduce the experiment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--3","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"hugging-face-hub-integration","text":"For co-training we combined multiple data sources into a single training dataset or ran multiple passesof training.","accordion":{"key":"hugging-face-use-cases","item":"hf-co-training-merge"}}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"hugging-face-hub-integration","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"hugging-face-hub-integration","source_sha256":"ebddda9cebdc6c1d187113b02cf6b9d4052c300250ae4016b775600ec063ce9d"}},{"id":"topic--04-lerobot--3:cue:21","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have seen how to inspect data. Now we will prepare the workspace for collecting it, starting with lighting, props, and camera views.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--04-lerobot--3","kind":"slide","index":12}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"04-lerobot.html","anchor":"community-resources","source_sha256":"5f9c900d3bd29972e35bd1d8548fdf8ecf9cc093671652240e40e76fd578bd10"}}]},{"id":"topic--07-using-workspace--1","index":12,"title":"Workspace · Check Lighting, Props, and Cameras","steps":[{"id":"topic--07-using-workspace--1:cue:0","title":"A workspace that produces observations","html":"<p>Now configure the physical workspace that produces our observations. Even with the same checkpoint, changes in lighting, object placement, or framing change the input. Compare the station with the reference scene.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--07-using-workspace--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-using-workspace.html","anchor":"using-the-workspace","source_sha256":"1984c989b9924cb5a7d397e6279abea13343e77a160ad80ed93d536dbfe70258"}},{"id":"topic--07-using-workspace--1:cue:5","title":"Lighting and layout","html":"<p>Unlock and power the light continuously. Choose brightness within the guide’s 50–100% range and keep it fixed during collection and evaluation. Facing the lightbox, place the rack on the left and the pickup area on the right, with one to three vials and empty rack slots.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--07-using-workspace--1","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"turn-on-the-light","text":"Press the light's power button.","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"turn-on-the-light","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-using-workspace.html","anchor":"turn-on-the-light","source_sha256":"180381cb5066511d9f9f2cd6d1905dab1c3e63c4ed0218ef31b7a909681aba62"}},{"id":"topic--07-using-workspace--1:cue:10","title":"Mounting and visibility","html":"<p>Check clamps and cables, and ensure the external view includes both rack and pickup area. The wrist view should be clear at close range. Stop the robot before reaching in to reposition objects.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--07-using-workspace--1","pane":3,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"check-before-moving","text":"Make sure the robot clamp is secure and cables are clear of the arm’s motion.","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"check-before-moving","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-using-workspace.html","anchor":"check-before-moving","source_sha256":"13017e6400cb105c21a669fdf214ec8a41c75b0f76ddf7f60f7a530f408796cb"}},{"id":"topic--07-using-workspace--1:cue:11","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Once the workspace is checked, we can identify the power connections and ports of both arms and prepare for calibration.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--07-using-workspace--1","kind":"slide","index":13}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"07-using-workspace.html","anchor":"using-the-workspace","source_sha256":"0f11adf59a0641a8833904744184048f7be97c6e4ae993104f657aa10cf9c0ba"}}]},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","index":13,"title":"Calibration Preparation · Power and Ports","steps":[{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:cue:0","title":"Power and device identity","html":"<p>Before calibration, inspect the arms and cables and distinguish the power supplies: 5 V for the leader and 12 V for the follower. Do not interchange them. Check USB connections and power LEDs, then identify each arm’s port.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"safety-guidelines","source_sha256":"bb5649d57ccca00725010aa1bb41fb2e26ec2f16bce751d4d2652e091b4a756a"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:cue:14","title":"Where commands run","html":"<p>Start the workshop container from the actual repository folder on the host, or attach with docker exec if it is already running. That folder supplies the source, datasets, and outputs mounts. Run subsequent device commands inside the container.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 2 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"run-the-docker-container-for-this-course","source_sha256":"6445e56d2dec69e92b1694ab9c42d13a54d8fd22fd4f9efe30754c1c81711a48"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:cue:19","title":"Map ports to roles","html":"<p>Follow the port finder to unplug the leader USB, identify the disappearing port, and reconnect it. Repeat for the follower. Use the actual results for each device rather than copying example ttyACM numbers.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":3,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":3,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":3,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"identify-the-teleop-arm-port","source_sha256":"fdd64e2820893faa25cbaf79cd0588d5e214d86daee46c557be0e35357aaa41c"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:cue:23","title":"Save ports and IDs","html":"<p>Store the leader port and ID in the TELEOP variables and the follower values in the ROBOT variables. Ports select devices; IDs distinguish calibration files. setenv persists values in docker/env, but recheck ports after reconnecting USB.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 2 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":3,"kind":"command","index":3,"blocks":"3"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":3,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":3,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"identify-the-teleop-arm-port","source_sha256":"5cba4e974f54810ffb33933d39cca17ae44edf487fb66ae90c98a24907a4b98d"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:cue:32","title":"Ready for calibration","html":"<p>Check that the printed ports and IDs match the physical arms. Continue with leader and follower calibration in the same container environment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 2 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":4,"kind":"command","index":2,"blocks":"2"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","pane":4,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":4,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"identify-the-robot-arm-port","source_sha256":"21dbf964d55ad6d575247d81974c77aee2058fc869a773179bf9daf391d55a09"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1:cue:35","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Each arm now has an identified port and ID. We will use these settings to calibrate the leader and follower in turn.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--1","kind":"slide","index":14}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"safety-guidelines","source_sha256":"e63932f5908dd7f342d9c6b7562db72995211d64ab231dd247b0b567c06424fb"}}]},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","index":14,"title":"Leader and Follower Calibration","steps":[{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2:cue:0","title":"What calibration aligns","html":"<p>Calibration maps encoder readings to physical link positions and motion ranges. For each arm, set the reference pose, record the range, then check the saved file and live state.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":-1,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"calibration-process","source_sha256":"90a2f9c918f0344ddbf63e1bd621b6d49b2cc6b23414eda103f868a0bdd4dc91"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2:cue:5","title":"Reference pose and range sweep","html":"<p>Run leader calibration and press Enter at the pictured midpoint pose. Set wrist roll accurately in the initial pose, but exclude it from the range sweep. Move the other joints through their full ranges individually, without cable or object interference.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"calibrate-the-leader-arm","source_sha256":"8cd2d3544ef120d40f0731fc955ce4d9663f04514db4a5b977a45cfd28177320"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2:cue:9","title":"Read the checker result","html":"<p>The checker compares saved ranges with the mean and ±2σ range of ten references per arm type. WARN flags differences to investigate. A static PASS does not verify current encoders; inspect live OUT OF RANGE results separately on the connected arm.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"calibrate-the-leader-arm","source_sha256":"cf84ec7c469bab748cad5f0035a2a3139f99dad3894ed62e3a13ab09df801aea"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2:cue:16","title":"Repeat for the follower","html":"<p>Repeat with ROBOT_PORT and ROBOT_ID for the follower. Correct initial wrist-roll orientation avoids reading wraparound. Verify the follower file and live checks so teleoperation interprets joint values consistently.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"calibrate-the-follower-arm","source_sha256":"7a723541a3f9bb04dd64194d66b827137c7c0f68d4bb2bac7d5027f21f8383fb"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2:cue:26","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>With both arms calibrated and checked, we will align the camera tilt and framing with the simulation views used for training.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--2","kind":"slide","index":15}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"calibration-process","source_sha256":"2d0586416ceeb137d866ded49c2ebc492e430695b4949be4cb005b9d984814ca"}}]},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3","index":15,"title":"Camera and Calibration · Final Checks","steps":[{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3:cue:0","title":"Align the camera","html":"<p>Next align the camera reference. Tilt the external camera about 54 degrees below horizontal to match the provided policy’s view. Zero a phone level on the table, then measure against the camera front.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"match-the-external-camera-tilt-to-simulation","source_sha256":"ddd203d5e5270e03facb7438519c24ae519fa2ad8dfc77548e8d2d92aed07816"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3:cue:3","title":"From angle to framing","html":"<p>The angle is a starting point for framing. Compare the robot, rack, and vial positions and field of view with simulation in the live image. Proceed once both the arm’s port, ID, and calibration and the camera view are aligned.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"match-the-external-camera-tilt-to-simulation","text":"Place the long edge of your phone on the table and check that it reads approximately 0°.","accordion":null}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"match-the-external-camera-tilt-to-simulation","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"match-the-external-camera-tilt-to-simulation","source_sha256":"d1e18d8f60760be73d7511c480dc02bea74b74865e01c26f63c8ea4c94c2e995"}},{"id":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3:cue:8","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>The arm and camera references are ready. We can now move the leader to operate the follower directly.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--07-calibrating-so101--3","kind":"slide","index":16}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"07-calibrating-so101.html","anchor":"match-the-external-camera-tilt-to-simulation","source_sha256":"40090afc4097c0b94e2f88eccf46cc8cfffaa7d0791e09d010ee00c2dd9d8282"}}]},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","index":16,"title":"Real Teleoperation · Safety and Basic Motion","steps":[{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--1:cue:0","title":"Prepare the first motion","html":"<p>For the first run, watch both arms directly to check that the follower responds correctly to the leader. Start with similar poses and clear the path the follower may take to match the leader.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","pane":-1,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"teleoperating","source_sha256":"60baf3437c950ba13c9d1754f7b8c3af4bd344cd3c6b5fe1584c3f6d467f340e"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--1:cue:8","title":"Safety and stopping","html":"<p>Keep hands away from powered follower joints and the gripper. Check finger clearance at the leader joints and cable routing. Stop unexpected motion with Ctrl+C and keep power disconnection accessible if needed.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","pane":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"safety","text":"Before powering on or running policies:","accordion":null}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"safety","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"safety","source_sha256":"2b4820ed40ced4094c1910841e151860cbc293fdb52158a85982011b6887f177"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--1:cue:12","title":"Basic teleoperation","html":"<p>Check types, ports, and IDs and run basic teleoperation without cameras. Move the leader slowly, verify follower response, and then try moving a vial. Stop before adding camera views for the next run.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"teleoperation-no-cameras","source_sha256":"48b8161ad8a3874a27f37249b322c29961f929f493e2d89c3b648fc62e8829d2"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--1:cue:14","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have tried operating the robot while watching it directly. Next, we will perform the same task using only the two camera views.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--1","kind":"slide","index":17}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"safety","source_sha256":"a30e62a90082dddf97208249e8554621dc9bf0643c9f514c3e1400b9b281fd27"}}]},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","index":17,"title":"Teleoperation with Two Cameras","steps":[{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--2:cue:0","title":"Use the policy’s views","html":"<p>Now teleoperate through the two cameras rather than looking directly at the workspace. The wrist view shows the grasp up close; the external view keeps the rack and workspace visible. The views complement each other when the wrist view becomes occluded after grasping.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"camera-setup","source_sha256":"14d24bfdd2e9e020619a10f121676694ccb2fee37636b8d275209fca7118fd09"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--2:cue:9","title":"Identify camera indices","html":"<p>Open the camera finder’s previews in outputs/captured_images on the host and map each index to its actual view. Assign distinct devices to wrist and external and recheck indices after reconnecting USB.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"finding-available-cameras","source_sha256":"5cd5586af7a787378464f4c82fa7c19694632553a36fd36dd928f2ae51410819"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--2:cue:22","title":"Operate and inspect through images","html":"<p>Run teleoperation with the verified camera settings and display_data. Check the assignments in Rerun, then attempt grasp and placement using only the images. Note where the target becomes occluded and stop with Ctrl+C when finished.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"teleoperation-with-cameras","source_sha256":"78bc5bb5ad3ce99643ea47fce66a05d0d906d82537a228bae7cabc40020c3f31"}},{"id":"topic--08-operating-so101--2:cue:31","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have experienced which information matters when operating through cameras. Next, we will examine how GR00T converts images, robot state, and a task instruction into actions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--08-operating-so101--2","kind":"slide","index":18}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"08-operating-so101.html","anchor":"camera-setup","source_sha256":"07f56a44f3d390d4ee7ea35b31d73332a57f6aeb079960599b490e4cb8e00ed5"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-groot--1","index":18,"title":"Isaac GR00T · Vision–Language–Action","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-groot--1:cue:0","title":"VLA inputs and outputs","html":"<p>GR00T predicts the actions we just chose from the two views. Vision provides the scene, language specifies the task, and robot state supplies current posture. The output is an executable action sequence.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"what-is-gr00t","source_sha256":"bae4d98bea6acfcc479d4750009a4f917d41d6feca56b0f2c02e0b521b85f93b"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--1:cue:12","title":"Learning the action mapping","html":"<p>A modular pipeline explicitly connects pose estimation, planning, and control. A VLA learns the observation-to-action relationship from demonstrations. Calibration remains necessary, and behavior depends on the situations represented in the data.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--1","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"modular-pipelines-and-learned-policies","text":"A modular robot pipeline might estimate an object’s pose, plan a motion, and send that plan to a controller. A VLA learn","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"modular-pipelines-and-learned-policies","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"modular-pipelines-and-learned-policies","source_sha256":"042ca7662165d74fa49c7cb98ca796e3c08ba2e89f9fcc5ecea4de2f3a7533e4"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--1:cue:14","title":"The data pyramid and our data","html":"<p>Human video and web data provide broad visual and language knowledge, while simulation and robot demonstrations connect it to action. We add SO-101 experience to this pretrained foundation for the vial task.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"the-data-pyramid","text":"Human Videos & Web Data: Learn human behavior and visual-language knowledge.","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"the-data-pyramid","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"the-data-pyramid","source_sha256":"b3d627352302f3fd92eb2b4c3a18f9cea10228640b92d09024e1e5d22ce65ce4"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--1:cue:17","title":"Training versus inference","html":"<p>Training changes model weights using a loss; hyperparameters such as batch size and learning rate govern that process. Post-training adapts pretrained weights to the task. Inference keeps those weights fixed and computes actions for new observations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--1","pane":4,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"defining-terminology","text":"Model weights, or parameters, are the numerical values learned inside a neural network. We use the two terms interchange","accordion":null}}],"pane":4,"anchor":"defining-terminology","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"defining-terminology","source_sha256":"5836a0a7304127044d97bcdbc333186e75819aadb342532819c6ffc1a4db8a98"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--1:cue:24","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have distinguished the VLA inputs, outputs, and learning stages. Next, we will connect these to the components used in GR00T training and inference.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--1","kind":"slide","index":19}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"what-is-gr00t","source_sha256":"edc58476abfc5c4aef85e3a05f361f9e23f5dbd28b35b95639941abe3bc3d661"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2","index":19,"title":"GR00T · Training, Inference, Architecture","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:cue:0","title":"Which weights learn?","html":"<p>The default recipe freezes vision and language weights while training action-related components. Frozen components still compute input features. Training vision as well changes memory and data requirements and should be compared as a separate experiment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"training-and-inference","source_sha256":"57a8e0affc1b67b5f25cd2f01627ec6318a65565402365764789fbd2ae945cf7"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:0:0:foundation","title":"Begin with pretrained model weights","html":"<p>Pre-training establishes representations that later robot adaptation can reuse. Broad visual and language data and robot experience play different roles; the diagram does not specify dataset proportions or a universal training order.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"foundation"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-training-stages.html","term":"foundation"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:0:0:demonstrations","title":"Sample examples of this robot and task","html":"<p>Post-training samples windows from demonstration episodes. Images, language, robot state, and future action targets must describe compatible task data.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"demonstrations"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-training-stages.html","term":"demonstrations"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:0:0:components","title":"Choose which weights can change","html":"<p>Frozen components still compute features, but the optimizer does not update their parameters. The base workshop example keeps vision and language frozen while training the action components.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"components"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-training-stages.html","term":"components"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:0:0:update","title":"Use loss to guide weight updates","html":"<p>The training objective supplies a loss from sampled predictions and targets. The optimizer updates trainable parameters; the learning-rate schedule controls the scale of those updates.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"update"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-training-stages.html","term":"update"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:0:0:unfreeze","title":"Optionally adapt visual features","html":"<p>Unfreezing permits the vision encoder to adapt to task images. It adds trainable parameters and memory use, and can increase overfitting risk.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"unfreeze"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-training-stages.html","term":"unfreeze"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:0:0:inference","title":"Use the adapted policy with fixed weights","html":"<p>Inference takes fresh images, instruction text, and robot state and predicts actions using the saved checkpoint and processors. Feedback changes the next observation, not the checkpoint weights.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"inference"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-training-stages.html","term":"inference"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:cue:12","title":"From backbone to action head","html":"<p>The GR00T 1.7 Cosmos-Reason2-2B backbone supplies image and instruction features. The action head combines these with robot state and generates action chunks through flow matching. This differs from the Cosmos video augmentation used later.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"architecture-overview","text":"GR00T 1.7 conditions action generation on images, language, and robot state. Its vision-language backbone is Cosmos-Reas","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"architecture-overview","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"architecture-overview","source_sha256":"b5fa83e464e3b7b07c98cf6a1b3bb51bdcef1e2322cf758c8b858854e5086d22"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:1:0:vision","title":"Vision: observe the task","html":"<p>The two views provide complementary evidence about the vial, rack, and gripper. The wrist view changes with the arm, so a different starting pose also changes what the policy sees.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"vision"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-observation-action.html","term":"vision"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:1:0:language","title":"Language: specify the task","html":"<p>The task instruction conditions the learned behavior alongside images and state. A language-conditioned model still needs suitable training coverage for the requested robot behavior.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"language"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-observation-action.html","term":"language"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:1:0:reason","title":"System 2: interpret images and language","html":"<p>Cosmos-Reason2-2B supplies the vision-language features used by the action head. “System 2” is a conceptual label for this perception and reasoning role, not a separate planner you program in this workshop.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"reason"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-observation-action.html","term":"reason"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:1:0:action","title":"System 1: generate a chunk of actions","html":"<p>The action head uses vision-language features and robot state to generate a sequence of joint commands. The saved processors convert the model representation into commands compatible with the robot.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"action"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-observation-action.html","term":"action"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:diagram:1:0:control","title":"Act, then observe the result","html":"<p>The rollout controller applies returned actions and obtains fresh observations for later predictions. Feedback changes the model’s inputs; ordinary inference does not update its weights.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"control"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/gr00t-observation-action.html","term":"control"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:cue:20","title":"Processors and control","html":"<p>Joint-state inputs require consistent ordering, units, and calibration. The processor converts model actions into executable commands, distinguishing relative arm targets from the absolute gripper target. New observations after execution inform the next chunk.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Robot state: where the arm is now","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"state"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"architecture-overview","source_sha256":"373f54594a9ed69130391dff887f699561fc83b617346407db4175f5c19f908f"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--2:cue:27","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have identified which architecture components are trained. Now we will examine one training step: from a batch, through the loss, to a weight update.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--2","kind":"slide","index":20}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"training-and-inference","source_sha256":"21adf4901b12a7c490169286ef1e93461655c1a684b2f9f951a9eac5209fad15"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3","index":20,"title":"One GR00T Training Step","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:cue:0","title":"Inputs to one update","html":"<p>A training step computes the batch loss and updates trainable weights once. Each sample is an observation and following action window, not a whole episode. Without accumulation, effective batch equals the per-process batch multiplied by the number of GPU processes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"what-happens-in-one-step-of-gr00t-training","source_sha256":"6cc07227c48c0643c017eadf20f186a60c53dcb4f9a0e499ec29c3db3f0b6c5f"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:edited:7654a288eb","title":"Baseline: one update","html":"<p>Start with batch 32 per GPU, one GPU process, and horizon 16. One update covers 32 windows and 512 target timesteps. These are sampled training targets, including repeats, rather than distinct episodes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Baseline: one update","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"values":{"#batch":32,"#gpus":1,"#horizon":16},"focus":".batch-controls"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:edited:d26c8c75ea","title":"What if batch doubles?","html":"<p>Keep GPU count and horizon fixed and change only batch to 64. The result is 64 windows and 1,024 target timesteps. Each update sees twice as many examples, but the update count is unchanged. Memory and runtime need separate measurement; this does not imply twice the success rate.</p>","actions":[{"label":"What if batch doubles?","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"values":{"#batch":64,"#gpus":1,"#horizon":16},"focus":".batch-controls"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:edited:74a625bd56","title":"Same target count, different temporal coverage","html":"<p>Now restore batch 32 and increase horizon to 32. The total is still 1,024 targets, but each of the 32 windows covers about 1.07 seconds of commands. Batch changes the number of examples; horizon changes temporal coverage per example. Equal products do not make the learning problem identical.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Same target count, different temporal coverage","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"values":{"#batch":32,"#gpus":1,"#horizon":32},"focus":".batch-controls"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:edited:1355f3326b","title":"Return to the baseline horizon","html":"<p>Restore horizon 16. At 30 Hz, nominal execution coverage is about 0.53 seconds; the first-to-last target timestamp span is 0.50 seconds. Next, see how these targets produce a loss and update the weights.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Return to the baseline horizon","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"values":{"#batch":32,"#gpus":1,"#horizon":16},"focus":".batch-controls"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:diagram:0:0:batch","title":"Batch size","html":"<p>Batch size counts sampled observation/action windows, not whole episodes. Each window contains camera images, the instruction, joint state, and an action target chunk starting at the sampled frame.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"batch"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/groot-training-step.html","term":"batch"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:diagram:0:0:targets","title":"Training horizon","html":"<p>The training action horizon is the number of action targets paired with an observation. The targets start at that frame: indices 0 through H−1.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"targets"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/groot-training-step.html","term":"targets"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:cue:7","title":"From loss to weight update","html":"<p>Flow matching mixes target actions and noise at a sampled flow time and computes masked MSE between predicted and target flows. Backpropagation and configured gradient clipping precede the optimizer update. Learning rate and schedule govern update size; checkpoint rollouts assess task performance.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Trainable components","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"loss"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"what-happens-in-one-step-of-gr00t-training","source_sha256":"1466741716fe54c00cc928d52dbc38e51e5769af6317b3d33aff989a2e66a254"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:diagram:0:0:update","title":"Learning rate","html":"<p>After backpropagation, the loop clips the gradient norm when configured, then the optimizer updates trainable weights. Learning rate scales this update together with the optimizer.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"update"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/groot-training-step.html","term":"update"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:cue:3","title":"Interpret training time","html":"<p>The guide records about 12.5 and 13.5 hours for two 20,000-step runs on one H100. These recipe-specific measurements include checkpoint saving and exclude data preparation and evaluation. Our hardware requires its own timing measurement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"what-happens-in-one-step-of-gr00t-training","text":"Our verified sim-only and mixed HG-DAgger 20,000-step runs took about 12½ and 13½ hours , respectively, on a single H100","accordion":null}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"what-happens-in-one-step-of-gr00t-training","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"what-happens-in-one-step-of-gr00t-training","source_sha256":"155247326bac713e3b2e4ed0773657c52f1a64b3bc2fe7a1474201c14768da11"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--3:cue:10","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>With one training step understood, we can move to a practical post-training recipe: choosing data and settings, then comparing results.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--3","kind":"slide","index":21}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"what-happens-in-one-step-of-gr00t-training","source_sha256":"20294cea8a889161b3b4e23d0d61b73263e92579e00e23112701befde3f3a68d"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4","index":21,"title":"Post-Training · Configuration and Validation","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:0","title":"The post-training setup","html":"<p>In class we evaluate prepared checkpoints; this section supplies the recipe for training your own. Stop collection and select the inspected, uploaded dataset, base model, model repository, and output directory. Keep inputs separate from outputs and retain the resolved configuration.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"reference-post-train-your-own-model","source_sha256":"9608f502d1de29e9ce30a6987ca463a6b9b39ab1d5cb2e29374278083d78e855"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:6","title":"Reference recipe and comparisons","html":"<p>The reference recipe uses 20,000 updates, checkpoints every 5,000 steps, BF16, and image augmentation. Batch is per GPU process, so changing batch or GPU count changes data exposure at fixed steps. The vision-unfrozen alternative also changes settings such as chunk length; its difference is not a vision-only effect.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":0,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"reference-post-train-your-own-model","source_sha256":"cb03cea89a50eab8d594f24c6a28e76d8585c54214c53b9acc20e969c9770f84"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:17","title":"Resume versus fresh fine-tuning","html":"<p>Resume restores weights, optimizer, scheduler, and the update counter. Starting a new run from checkpoint weights creates a fresh optimizer and schedule and counts from zero. Thus, 20,000 fresh updates from 50,000-step weights and resuming toward a total of 70,000 have different starting states.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","text":"Choose What to Restore","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","source_sha256":"1c9612b24658fb97ebad8b14cca4332f901683365ebaa8b1c57cfb64069dcbd7"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:22","title":"Checkpoint compatibility","html":"<p>Load checkpoint weights together with configuration and processors. Camera keys, joint representation, calibration, and base revision must agree. The same gripper percentage can represent different openings under different calibration; any required mapping must cover observations before normalization and actions after decoding.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","text":"A gripper percentage can mean a different physical opening when its encoder calibration differs. Collection and simulati","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","source_sha256":"a23d5f5f7b4b6b92b8fd389fb6edd042090938b71010f4629be7cb1a7963d006"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:27","title":"Mixture and actual sampling","html":"<p>Stored episode proportions can differ from training sampling proportions. The workshop path shuffles eligible frames after merging, so longer episodes contribute more examples. Enforcing a particular sim/real mixture requires a source-aware sampler.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","text":"Two useful experiments are starting from the simulation-trained checkpoint and continuing on mixed sim/real data, or sta","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","source_sha256":"6f9f921b2dfbc2808f3650791010d5f6e3ecc3985b8a14e86c847f57b5d753e4"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:30","title":"Measure improvement","html":"<p>Held-out validation measures predictions on unseen demonstrations; rollout measures task completion. Keep test data out of normalization statistics as well. If loss falls while unfamiliar positions fail, inspect starting poses and coverage before adding updates, and compare intermediate checkpoints under matched conditions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","text":"Keep some demonstrations aside for testing: never use them to train the model or calculate the normalization statistics ","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"experiment-technique-continue-training-a-checkpoint","source_sha256":"9563f8135cc2c58b5e751ece4bcd015077a6f057af0c06d68a63971f32878cbd"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:video:2:0","title":"Look beyond the loss","html":"<p>In this example, the policy waits until the vial is repositioned near the gripper. Evaluation with changed object positions helps reveal whether it relies on familiar states.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":2,"media":0}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--10-groot--4","pane":2,"media":0,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--4:cue:42","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>To interpret the training recipe, we also need to distinguish the action settings. Next, we will look at action representations and prediction versus execution lengths.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--4","kind":"slide","index":22}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"reference-post-train-your-own-model","source_sha256":"c7e3e7dee91bf4d4a3f4262da4676c0ad2a72feabcd5893a7e873f526429d0a0"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5","index":22,"title":"Action Space · Prediction and Execution","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:cue:0","title":"What an action represents","html":"<p>Joint actions specify joint targets directly; end-effector actions use a control layer such as IK to convert hand targets into joint commands. Today’s joint-position actions use arm targets relative to the observed state and an absolute gripper target.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"action-representations","source_sha256":"a56a9b5432b138f4277855019f9798b11b373be1c48586f7a0d371cdbfe98866"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:cue:10","title":"Prediction and execution lengths","html":"<p>chunk_size sets the training target length, n_action_steps limits the policy’s returned sequence, and client or engine horizon settings govern execution. We compare with a 16-action rollout. Selecting the RTC engine with enabled=false leaves delay compensation off.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 5 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":0,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"action-representations","source_sha256":"3cd980f258825625fbd5e3dc9e987c0875b08a0d751924c178279c2088790812"}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:d96efa7a30","title":"Training baseline","html":"<p>Before comparing execution lengths, check training exposure. Batch 32, one GPU, and 20,000 updates give 640,000 sampled windows. Repeated samples count again; this is not 640,000 new examples.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Training baseline","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-batch":32,"#groot-gpus":1,"#groot-steps":20000},"tab":"groot-training-tab","focus":"#groot-training"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:6cb131690b","title":"Change batch, keep updates","html":"<p>Changing batch to 64 gives 1,280,000 windows over the same 20,000 updates. Equal update counts can mean different data exposure. This is why comparisons must record batch and GPU count as well.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Change batch, keep updates","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-batch":64,"#groot-gpus":1,"#groot-steps":20000},"tab":"groot-training-tab","focus":"#groot-training"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:0f0dcea6c4","title":"Match total exposure","html":"<p>Reduce updates to 10,000 at batch 64 and total exposure returns to 640,000 windows. Exposure matches the baseline, but there are half as many weight updates. Schedules and gradient composition differ, so performance and runtime need not match. Now switch to inference with fixed weights.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Match total exposure","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-batch":64,"#groot-gpus":1,"#groot-steps":10000},"tab":"groot-training-tab","focus":"#groot-training"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:74b3176945","title":"Execution horizon 16","html":"<p>With the baseline execution horizon of 16 at 30 Hz, a new prediction is used about every 533 ms, or 1.88 times per second. The robot still receives 30 commands per second. These nominal intervals exclude inference and network delay.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Execution horizon 16","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-batch":32,"#groot-gpus":1,"#groot-steps":20000,"#groot-horizon":16},"tab":"groot-inference-tab","focus":"#groot-inference"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:b8e8b175aa","title":"Reconsider every 8 actions","html":"<p>Reduce only the execution horizon to 8: about 267 ms between predictions, or 3.75 predictions per second. There is an earlier opportunity to use a new observation, at twice the inference request rate. The robot command rate does not become 60 Hz.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Reconsider every 8 actions","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-horizon":8},"tab":"groot-inference-tab","focus":"#groot-inference"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:e8b45e3b7f","title":"Compare 4 actions and interpret","html":"<p>Horizon 4 gives about 133 ms and 7.50 predictions per second—four times the baseline request rate. Better responsiveness also requires new predictions to be ready in time. Shorter is not always better: compare actual latency and task success together.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Compare 4 actions and interpret","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-horizon":4},"tab":"groot-inference-tab","focus":"#groot-inference"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:edited:8b775b995a","title":"Restore the baseline for the exercise","html":"<p>Restore horizon 16. These controls change only the teaching illustration, not the training configuration or robot controller. Next, record the observations and actions in simulation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Restore the baseline for the exercise","action":{"kind":"widget","slide":"topic--10-groot--5","pane":1,"media":0,"values":{"#groot-batch":32,"#groot-gpus":1,"#groot-steps":20000,"#groot-horizon":16},"tab":"groot-inference-tab","focus":"#groot-inference"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-edition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":""}},{"id":"topic--10-groot--5:cue:19","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We now understand the inputs and action targets used to train GR00T. Next, we will create this data through teleoperation in simulation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-groot--5","kind":"slide","index":23}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-groot.html","anchor":"action-space-and-control","source_sha256":"8f941183d89d998861b0085a41931f6d3caff16fd9c4cbf9c6fa1cdd395c9b86"}}]},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","index":23,"title":"Sim Teleoperation · Launch and Camera Views","steps":[{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1:cue:0","title":"Physical leader, simulated follower","html":"<p>We now use the physical leader to move the simulated SO-101. Stop real teleoperation and use the calibrated leader’s port and ID. The follower is simulated, but the leader still needs a safe physical operating area.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"teleoperation-collecting-human-demonstrations","source_sha256":"4e93cf0a2bac917c71b87bf0f4c7054600b18c0cbc35151d438f68eca558f34c"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1:cue:17","title":"Start the task and cameras","html":"<p>Specify the DR task, cameras all, and both newton and ovrtx_renderer presets. The task ID selects the scene and randomization; camera and renderer settings provide image inputs. Check motion and both views before enabling recording.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"practice-teleoperation-in-simulation","source_sha256":"feba599bdf1f2dd112be38b02c5c831bfd20e3b0bccbec614ee67026147103e3"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1:cue:21","title":"Practice in the browser","html":"<p>Connect one browser tab to localhost:8090 on the host. With that tab focused, use R to reset the layout and perform the task from the camera views. These keyboard controls belong to the browser path and do not carry over to Rerun or headless execution.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"set-up-the-camera-display","text":"Open http://localhost:8090 in the host browser. Connect only one browser tab to the viewer at a time.","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"set-up-the-camera-display","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"set-up-the-camera-display","source_sha256":"c786bbeef2d85ba1f5b900e1a5a8f4043d5f1381fd02fbd58f8879a9b852a7aa"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1:cue:23","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have checked the leader response and both camera views in simulation. Next, we will save these motions as episodes and inspect the recordings.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--1","kind":"slide","index":24}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"teleoperation-collecting-human-demonstrations","source_sha256":"959cb90145a62cc2477ef8e01da099d52716a699aa1eb0faa6afd7540c48c8a3"}}]},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","index":24,"title":"Demonstrations · Record, Review, and Expand","steps":[{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:0","title":"The recorded time relationship","html":"<p>Episodes contain cameras, joint state, actions, instructions, and timestamps. State is the measured posture; action is the target about to be applied. Record current observations with subsequent commands rather than treating state and action as identical.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"inside-a-demonstration","source_sha256":"5c7a7f46d015c4eda71b98a8a26229abbae902243a7c1c8c38e294ac8cf61c51"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:diagram:0:0:images","title":"What the robot saw","html":"<p>An observation contains the wrist and external camera views. Each camera’s name, mount pose, field of view, and frame timing should match between recording and policy use.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"images"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/lerobot-episode.html","term":"images"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:diagram:0:0:state","title":"Where the robot was","html":"<p>Joint state records the robot’s measured joint positions. Joint state may differ from the commanded state because motion takes time and real actuators do not track commands perfectly.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"state"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/lerobot-episode.html","term":"state"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:diagram:0:0:action","title":"What was commanded","html":"<p>An action is the joint command sent after the observation is captured: from the leader arm during recording, and from the policy during evaluation. Its joint order, units and calibration must agree with the robot interface and the training setup.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"action"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/lerobot-episode.html","term":"action"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:diagram:0:0:episode","title":"Keep the sequence together","html":"<p>Timestamps and episode boundaries preserve the sequence of the task. The instruction describes the task performed in that sequence.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"episode"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/lerobot-episode.html","term":"episode"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:17","title":"Choose storage and record","html":"<p>Set HF_USER and repo_id and add the task instruction to start recording into datasets/&lt;repo_id&gt;. Use resume to append or a new repo_id for a separate experiment. Local collection is separate from Hub authentication and upload.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"record-demonstrations","source_sha256":"ad631deffd58dfd3093803427018289d44e3550b69bad56e401878056e726309"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:21","title":"Saving versus resetting","html":"<p>With browser focus, Space starts a take and Space again saves it. C discards it; R discards and resets the environment. Saving runs in the background, so wait for completion and allow shutdown and encoding to finish at session end.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"record-demonstrations","text":"Open http://localhost:8090. Connect only one browser tab to the viewer at a time.","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"record-demonstrations","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"record-demonstrations","source_sha256":"7afdd0eddb652d433733e694d7e63a14ca29a1b9c26d386179e0e4cd6ea7813a"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:26","title":"Inspect before sharing","html":"<p>Open the repo ID, local root, and episode index in the Visualizer to inspect timing, grasp, and placement. Upload the inspected local dataset under the matching Hub namespace. After updates, ensure the v3.0 tag points to the intended revision.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":3,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":3,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":3,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"review-and-upload-collected-data","source_sha256":"1e3f9ea69e5452ec618b87f9819dc7b8c9e798a22b73511e429afabe0b030f25"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:39","title":"Scale with scripted collection","html":"<p>vials_state_machine replaces the leader with a scripted controller. Inspect a preview before recording. A target of five means five successful episodes; failed attempts are excluded. Collection does not use Space/C, and the dataset is exported after reaching the target, so allow export to finish.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 2 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":4,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":4,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":4,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"optional-automated-data-collection","source_sha256":"87768f01ca352d43769aa2f3c3f7e401f85a0a2cf90ceef5be9e88319a6fbb5b"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:45","title":"Choose the collection method","html":"<p>Simulation supports repeated resets and layouts; real data captures actual cameras, contact, and actuator responses. Scripted and human collection also provide different motions. Add data that fills missing positions, views, and behaviors rather than merely increasing counts.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 5 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":4,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","pane":4,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":4,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"optional-automated-data-collection","source_sha256":"a656a3b912b221accfb65852aacfb504975f881f6432ea0a72be70768f8e152d"}},{"id":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2:cue:52","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have covered collection and inspection. Next is domain randomization: collecting experience of the same task under more varied conditions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--08-simulation-teleoperation--2","kind":"slide","index":25}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"08-simulation-teleoperation.html","anchor":"recording-demonstrations","source_sha256":"a8b1ffb4d9ee283bd0c3712ac4522afb8728c134d8acadce3658d67500c526ff"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","index":25,"title":"Domain Randomization · Learning to Handle Variation","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:cue:0","title":"The mismatch DR addresses","html":"<p>Domain Randomization samples plausible operating conditions in simulation and trains the policy on those varied examples. It reduces dependence on a single scene, while variation must remain within a range where the task is feasible.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"what-is-domain-randomization","source_sha256":"297284c03b69396ca07b0cd75aff9a83b245e966b39323b93117ed4d2763098e"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:cue:8","title":"Choose what varies","html":"<p>Lighting and materials change appearance; camera and base tilt change observation geometry. Dynamics such as friction or delay can also be randomized, but check which terms are active in this task. The DR label does not imply that every parameter varies.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 1. Vary conditions","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"vary"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"what-is-domain-randomization","source_sha256":"8b981e04e1071d266b193b4a2f140765d1e9f434c15e2c270e9ea7a090b81316"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:diagram:0:0:record","title":"2. Record examples","html":"<p>Collect successful demonstrations across those resets. The recorded images, joint states, and actions become learning examples.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"record"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/domain-randomization.html","term":"record"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:cue:10","title":"How variation reaches learning","html":"<p>Reset the conditions, collect demonstrations in those scenes, and use them for post-training. This differs from image transforms applied during training, and visual variation alone does not add recovery actions. Compare sim and real rollouts with matched starting conditions and horizons.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 3. Post-train","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"train"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"what-is-domain-randomization","source_sha256":"69318bb52cff8324ffb7100c43739dc6bc2713c717ae7539937af1996f6ebdaa"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:diagram:0:0:test","title":"4. Test transfer","html":"<p>Compare policies under documented evaluation conditions. Keep the checkpoint, instruction, action horizon, and relevant starting conditions traceable.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"test"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/domain-randomization.html","term":"test"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1:cue:14","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Let us check this principle in the images themselves, comparing what changes in the external and wrist views.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--1","kind":"slide","index":26}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"what-is-domain-randomization","source_sha256":"2e8fc7134b9da2dda35c7c7b306b290a6efdfc84c8c98d7f8e1b4870c6389f5a"}}]},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2","index":26,"title":"Domain Randomization · Camera Comparison","steps":[{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2:cue:0","title":"Variation in both cameras","html":"<p>The comparison shows visual differences among standard, DR, Perception-DR, and real references. GIFs span resets and static references use other layouts, so these are not synchronized frame pairs. Distinguish capture labels from the current checkout’s active settings.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"test-domain-randomization","source_sha256":"ecfbaf2fdb6fbfb171785ba578f6adc8d7c7f35b07a92c3c4991f6de3f6c445e"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2:cue:9","title":"Active parameter ranges","html":"<p>Current settings include base tilt from −3 to +6 degrees and external-camera tilt within ±5 degrees of nominal. Some camera positions and wrist calibration remain fixed. After changing ranges, inspect both views across resets for visibility and task feasibility.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 9 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2","pane":1,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"code-tour-domain-randomization-implementation","source_sha256":"61ea4a65b2c2e1ef5a11ae1f108e67efb5e8f71829cc653bbe17ce29eb4e9792"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2:cue:12","title":"From settings to actual images","html":"<p>ovrtx renders a separately loaded scene, so USD changes must reach the renderer through the workshop write helpers. Verify camera outputs after restart and warmup before concluding that recordings and policy inputs contain the change.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"code-tour-domain-randomization-implementation","text":"The implementations live in source/sim_to_real_so101/mdp/resets.py . ovrtx loads its scene separately, so visual changes","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"code-tour-domain-randomization-implementation","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"code-tour-domain-randomization-implementation","source_sha256":"f87540ff313e421315559173b694093dc2553f81f94ea1ee47767d39de9190f3"}},{"id":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2:cue:15","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have examined the variation in the camera images. Next, we will look at the software stack that produces the scene and observations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--10-domain-randomization--2","kind":"slide","index":27}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"10-domain-randomization.html","anchor":"domain-randomization-in-simulation","source_sha256":"947d08958be79a0884f5382e54491e3b2b0bfb5007162a950d897e9d1b449d13"}}]},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1","index":27,"title":"Workshop Architecture · Connecting the Components","steps":[{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:cue:0","title":"Layers connecting collection and evaluation","html":"<p>The application connects LeRobot, Isaac Lab, Newton, and rendering libraries. Workshop code coordinates task execution, recording, and evaluation, while the bridge converts LeRobot joint representations to simulation representations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"how-the-pieces-fit-together","source_sha256":"4849760fafa6f213dd91fa56a92f0293204717f3e4abfc709cafe2bce10188d5"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:diagram:0:0:workshop","title":"Workshop code","html":"<p>Combines the task configuration, controller, recording, and evaluation. The bridge converts LeRobot commands into simulation joint commands and converts observations back for recording or inference.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"workshop"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/workshop-code-stack.html","term":"workshop"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:diagram:0:0:lerobot","title":"LeRobot","html":"<p>Provides teleoperator and robot interfaces, the dataset format, and policy tools. During collection the leader supplies actions; during evaluation a policy supplies them.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"lerobot"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/workshop-code-stack.html","term":"lerobot"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:cue:8","title":"From scene to observation","html":"<p>Isaac Lab combines assets, actions, observations, resets, and termination into an environment. Newton updates physical state; ovstage and ovrtx produce camera images from the scene. ovstream delivers those images to the browser.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Isaac Lab","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"lab"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"how-the-pieces-fit-together","source_sha256":"369d04788281f7920fe5124ec2b42faa6cd1c3a1bf973ffb0e6e00044fdf31f7"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:diagram:0:0:usd","title":"USD assets","html":"<p>Describe the workstation geometry, materials, camera properties, and physical properties. Isaac Lab loads these assets for simulation, and the Omniverse libraries manage their rendered scene.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"usd"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/workshop-code-stack.html","term":"usd"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:diagram:0:0:newton","title":"Newton","html":"<p>Advances robot and object dynamics using the configured MJWarp solver and collision pipeline. Updated poses drive the simulated scene and become joint observations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"newton"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/workshop-code-stack.html","term":"newton"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:diagram:0:0:ov","title":"Omniverse libraries","html":"<p>ovstage manages the USD scene used for rendering. ovrtx renders wrist and external RGB images from the updated scene. ovstream takes GPU renders and stream them to the browser. Isaac Lab exposes those camera results as observations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"ov"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/workshop-code-stack.html","term":"ov"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:cue:12","title":"Boundaries for diagnosis","html":"<p>Image generation, browser delivery, and dataset recording are separate stages. Diagnose the first boundary where input or output differs from expectation. Trace joint issues through the bridge and action terms, and image issues through camera, renderer, and streaming stages.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Clear diagram selection","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":""}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"how-the-pieces-fit-together","source_sha256":"41641437364964d35e812a8212dcc081259a540f7d2b4c2de70d7625d82bd208"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--1:cue:13","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>The Isaac Lab environment organizes the task within this stack. Next, we will trace how its configuration and cameras connect to execution.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--1","kind":"slide","index":28}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"how-the-pieces-fit-together","source_sha256":"9841a5a89301d23bfc64449c84a5925d2b332472b8b3970927622b37fe25cd9d"}}]},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2","index":28,"title":"Isaac Lab · Environments and Cameras","steps":[{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:cue:0","title":"What the environment configures","html":"<p>The task ID selects an EnvironmentConfig connecting scene, physics, actions, observations, events, and termination. Changing the action source among leader, scripted controller, and policy lets us collect and evaluate within the same task.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"isaac-lab-environment-configuration","source_sha256":"7d7cee7e35d44cad592ca20226ca3fc0546ea08cc52548f2faa2f89e4d45bc2f"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-0","title":"Articulation / robot","html":"<p>Joints, actuators, physical properties.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-0"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-0"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-1","title":"Assets","html":"<p>Static and dynamic objects, lights and materials.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-1"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-1"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-2","title":"Sensors","html":"<p>Cameras, contact sensors, etc..</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-2"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-2"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-3","title":"Actions","html":"<p>Action terms map controller outputs into commands for the simulated robot.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-3"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-3"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-4","title":"Scene","html":"<p>Asset placement and parallel environments.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-4"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-4"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:cue:11","title":"Timing and events","html":"<p>With physics dt of 1/60 second and decimation of two, each control step advances physics twice, yielding 30 Hz control. Startup, reset, and periodic events run at different times. Termination indicates an episode ending, not necessarily success.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Simulation settings","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"node-5"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"isaac-lab-environment-configuration","source_sha256":"5198c8e4446ebf517d9bc13f9ef49dbc8709f71477d89fc446c58c21e15136bb"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-6","title":"Events","html":"<p>Startup terms prepare the scene; reset terms vary a new episode; periodic terms run at configured intervals.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-6"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-6"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-7","title":"Observations","html":"<p>Build inputs from state and sensor data.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-7"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-7"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-8","title":"Terminations","html":"<p>Success, failure, and time limits determine when an episode ends. An ending alone is not evidence of success.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-8"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-8"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:cue:15","title":"Observation and learning boundaries","html":"<p>The observation manager constructs inputs from selected sensors and state; GR00T does not receive every scene variable. The presence of reward terms does not make today’s demonstration recording reward-driven reinforcement learning.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Rewards","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"node-9"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"isaac-lab-environment-configuration","source_sha256":"394adaa93d201b750eab92d2a2c57f77300e19509ad2559c21fb92d0749004bf"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-10","title":"Environment","html":"<p>Coordinates scene, managers, stepping, and episode resets.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-10"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-10"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:0:0:node-11","title":"Policy","html":"<p>During evaluation the policy predicts actions from observations. During collection a teleoperator or scripted controller can supply actions instead.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-11"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-manager-based.html","term":"node-11"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:1:0:node-0","title":"Environment","html":"<p>Provides the scene and its current state.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-0"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-camera-ovrtx.html","term":"node-0"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:cue:24","title":"What camera settings mean","html":"<p>Camera configuration combines pose, lens, resolution, update period, and renderer. The example produces 640×480 RGB every 1/30 second. These images feed recording and policy inference as well as display, so both view and timing must agree.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Camera configuration","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"node-1"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"camera-sensor","source_sha256":"254f9da9238a01c4f37fe6d536f07a517337a5a1cc20cdde90f477be5adfa536"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:1:0:node-2","title":"ovrtx renderer","html":"<p>ovrtx renders the current scene from the requested camera pose and returns RGB images.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-2"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-camera-ovrtx.html","term":"node-2"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:1:0:node-3","title":"Camera sensor","html":"<p>The camera sensor connects its configured view and update timing to rendering, then exposes the resulting image data.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-3"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-camera-ovrtx.html","term":"node-3"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:diagram:1:0:node-4","title":"Observations","html":"<p>Camera images available to the policy.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-4"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-camera-ovrtx.html","term":"node-4"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--2:cue:29","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have seen how the environment and cameras are configured. Next, we will connect reset, observation computation, and recording through one control loop.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--2","kind":"slide","index":29}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"isaac-lab-environment-configuration","source_sha256":"5cd20bdb97153ef1533bbdf16c745b433811e92886d4139c3841e957f1c61564"}}]},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3","index":29,"title":"Reset, Observation, Recording","steps":[{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:cue:0","title":"Reset creates a new episode","html":"<p>Reset events create the layout of a new episode by sampling rack and vial positions within configured ranges. They do not reposition objects every control step. Positions use meters and yaw uses radians.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"reset-terms","source_sha256":"3db11645adcef95afa8eb5cd6d8854feb8a58f69af8e098f4cc1456e9dcdd09f"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:0:0:node-0","title":"Reset event configuration","html":"<p>Reset event configuration specifies what placement changes at the start of an episode. The configured function and sampling ranges are applied during reset.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-0"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-rack-vial-randomization.html","term":"node-0"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:0:0:node-1","title":"Initial reset","html":"<p>Initialize the scene before the first control step, including the configured rack and vial placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-1"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-rack-vial-randomization.html","term":"node-1"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:0:0:node-2","title":"Environment step","html":"<p>An environment step applies actions and advances the simulation. Distinguish this repeated control step from resetting for a new episode.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-2"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-rack-vial-randomization.html","term":"node-2"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:0:0:node-3","title":"Event manager","html":"<p>A reset event runs for the environments being reset, not on every ordinary control step.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-3"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-rack-vial-randomization.html","term":"node-3"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:0:0:node-4","title":"Randomize placement","html":"<p>The reset function samples rack placement using configured ranges. Here x and y are in meters and yaw is in radians.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"node-4"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-rack-vial-randomization.html","term":"node-4"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:cue:12","title":"From contact to grasp signal","html":"<p>Contact force and vial height form the vial_grasped signal. This controller heuristic serves a different role from GR00T’s RGB and joint inputs. A true flag does not establish stable grasping or completed rack placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"grasp-observation","text":"Observation terms can turn sensor readings into useful task signals. The workshop combines contact force with vial heigh","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"grasp-observation","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"grasp-observation","source_sha256":"463beb78e9853351b3c5156009b0caadbddfe856f0034f7a52e13195ecbf14da"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:1:0:node-0","title":"Observation configuration","html":"<p>Observation configuration defines the terms to compute and how they are grouped. It configures the vial-grasp term to read the required sensors and state.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-0"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-vial-grasp-observation.html","term":"node-0"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:1:0:node-1","title":"Contact sensor","html":"<p>Contact readings provide evidence for the grasp check. Contact alone is not a successful placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-1"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-vial-grasp-observation.html","term":"node-1"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:1:0:node-2","title":"Environment step","html":"<p>The environment step updates the scene and sensors. Observations read that updated state to provide information for the next decision.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-2"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-vial-grasp-observation.html","term":"node-2"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:1:0:node-3","title":"Observation manager","html":"<p>Compute configured observation terms from the updated scene and sensor data.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-3"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-vial-grasp-observation.html","term":"node-3"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:1:0:node-4","title":"Grasp check","html":"<p>The workshop uses contact force and vial height to acquire a grasp flag, then keeps it while contact persists. This heuristic is not proof of a stable grasp.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-4"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-vial-grasp-observation.html","term":"node-4"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:1:0:node-5","title":"Observations","html":"<p>vial_grasped is the computed grasp indicator. This flag alone does not establish successful rack placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"node-5"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-vial-grasp-observation.html","term":"node-5"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:cue:23","title":"Recorder order and units","html":"<p>The loop reads leader input, converts it through the bridge, and records current images and state with the pending action. Physics and rendering then produce the next observation. Distinguish simulation radians from dataset arm degrees and gripper percentages to preserve motion meaning.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"lerobot-recorder","text":"During teleoperation, the leader supplies the action. Record the current images and joint state with that action before ","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"lerobot-recorder","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"lerobot-recorder","source_sha256":"7fb64e194798ab9d68b0fdef13d164a78bd571ab77f097c40f5f33f8e8fda3f1"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:2:0:node-0","title":"Real leader arm","html":"<p>The leader arm supplies the human-intended joint commands. Pair these inputs with the current simulation observations to record demonstrations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"node-0"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-lerobot-recording.html","term":"node-0"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:2:0:node-1","title":"lerobot_interface","html":"<p>Read the leader arm and bridge its joint values to the simulated action representation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"node-1"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-lerobot-recording.html","term":"node-1"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:2:0:node-2","title":"lerobot_agent","html":"<p>Read the action, record it with the current observation, then advance simulation. The rate limiter paces the next iteration.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"node-2"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-lerobot-recording.html","term":"node-2"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:2:0:node-3","title":"Environment","html":"<p>Simulated robot + cameras.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"node-3"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-lerobot-recording.html","term":"node-3"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:2:0:node-4","title":"LeRobot recorder","html":"<p>Record the current joint state and camera images together with the action applied next, before advancing simulation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"node-4"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-lerobot-recording.html","term":"node-4"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:diagram:2:0:node-5","title":"LeRobot dataset","html":"<p>Save synchronized observations and actions as an episode for later review and training.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"node-5"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/isaac-lab-lerobot-recording.html","term":"node-5"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:cue:36","title":"What changes during evaluation","html":"<p>Evaluation replaces the leader with the policy. The timing relationship remains the same: the current observation informs the next action. This shared path connects recorded demonstrations with policy execution.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","pane":3,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"follow-one-control-step","text":"During policy evaluation, the policy supplies actions instead of the leader. The same simulation scene and task checks t","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"follow-one-control-step","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"follow-one-control-step","source_sha256":"af39bbd09610af9434f61d0680d3cc7dea7ede27baa6e6804c841c551476f68c"}},{"id":"topic--09-code-tour--3:cue:37","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have traced how actions and observations are generated and recorded in simulation. After a short break, we will look at expanding demonstrations with a state machine.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--09-code-tour--3","kind":"slide","index":30}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"09-code-tour.html","anchor":"reset-terms","source_sha256":"7b3e9e93f84a8ac4544c068ed2e34a067e26ac64fed3a845abb544fa10d7784b"}}]},{"id":"qa-1150--start","index":30,"title":"Break","steps":[{"id":"qa-1150--start:cue:0","title":"Break","html":"<p>We will take a short break, then turn demonstrated motions into rules for scaling collection.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"qa-1150--start","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"1598e3224e5cf603a9cde6e0a02329064ee31de142e047a54f177abeba96e3b8"}},{"id":"qa-1150--start:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Let us resume with the process of analyzing human demonstrations and expressing their motions as a state machine.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"qa-1150--start","kind":"slide","index":31}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"898eeffc6a3820eb996807dcc2c5e917f1f62e83a04192203b61fb7a5519de75"}}]},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1","index":31,"title":"From Demonstrations to a State Machine","steps":[{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1:cue:0","title":"Extract motion rules","html":"<p>A state machine divides the task into phases such as approach, grasp, transport, insertion, and release, with transition conditions. Extracting motion and timing rules from real demonstrations supports repeated simulation collection in new layouts.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"from-a-few-demonstrations-to-new-layouts","source_sha256":"4d2717db32785043ba6e37db33e0c397e129847f949423ad89a60d0e1b68ff33"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1","pane":0,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1:cue:7","title":"Implement with matched conditions","html":"<p>This example starts from ten real demonstrations. The agent analyzes trajectories, speeds, and grasp timing without changing the scene to make success easier. Compare the generated controller under matched scene and camera conditions to assess motion improvements.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"start-with-a-focused-prompt","text":"We seed the process with 10 real demonstrations from sreetz-nv/so101_real_manual . The prompt keeps the agent focused on","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"start-with-a-focused-prompt","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"start-with-a-focused-prompt","source_sha256":"c1636f8f12ec06db37daf7ecc68cc886dd42d6b92143ddcea5152667de6ccbbf"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1:cue:9","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Let&#x27;s see what the actual comparison identified for revision.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--1","kind":"slide","index":32}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"from-a-few-demonstrations-to-new-layouts","source_sha256":"121f7d4802b0273589e4a1b3c3fdea6e67654327c4f62a42551b02dc2c251b15"}}]},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","index":32,"title":"Analyzing and Improving Motion","steps":[{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2:cue:0","title":"From success to motion quality","html":"<p>Once a state machine completes the task, compare its motion with real demonstrations. Starting pose, gripper path, grasp height, and lift timing after closure determine the training examples it produces.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"analyze-the-motion-then-refine-it","source_sha256":"9c821c902d0f582ad8a2571afd60d694fa6a3c32f94abb40414a9f52f18797d1"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2:video:0:1","title":"Compare demonstrated and generated motion","html":"<p>Compare the real demonstration with the baseline and refined state machines. Watch the approach path and when lifting begins after gripper closure to identify the motion changes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":0,"media":1}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":0,"media":1,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2:cue:7","title":"Pose and visual input","html":"<p>Varied object positions do not broaden state coverage if the arm always starts in one pose. Grasp height also changes how the cap and body appear in the wrist image. Compare starting and grasp poses separately.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"match-the-starting-pose-distribution","text":"A state machine that always starts in one pose can produce a narrow training distribution, even when the vial and rack p","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"match-the-starting-pose-distribution","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"match-the-starting-pose-distribution","source_sha256":"738088630d2fd4e28db589cadda5027b83a2a9227d4c24509e1b75654e9773fa"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":1,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2:cue:13","title":"Why timing matters","html":"<p>Aligning traces at gripper closure reveals when lifting starts. Repeated unnecessary pauses accumulate stationary targets for similar observations. In new layouts, verify that motion adapts to object relationships instead of replaying a fixed trajectory.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 4","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":1,"media":3,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":1,"media":3,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","pane":1,"media":3,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"lessons-learned","source_sha256":"2b64f2ea232e35a63dcdb157f20649870d7e087109de9a0023674cfc0bfd4418"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2:cue:14","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have established how to inspect generated motion. Next, we will compare other ways to expand demonstrations, using AutoData and an agent.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--2","kind":"slide","index":33}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"analyze-the-motion-then-refine-it","source_sha256":"aa6671e0944a229b7221834e16e16408c508ea7b4b92235ff0986342b585188b"}}]},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","index":33,"title":"Expanding Demonstrations · AutoData and Agents","steps":[{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:cue:0","title":"Three ways to scale data","html":"<p>A state machine encodes motion rules. AutoData transforms and recombines object-relative segments from annotated seed demonstrations. An agent acting directly as controller instead chooses the next action from each observation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"another-way-to-scale-demonstrations-isaac-autodata","source_sha256":"86a7c5e62c52687a7d6d8383a6387f57390207113d113e1f8e7745f01eb5764e"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:cue:8","title":"One agent decision","html":"<p>This agent receives two images, joint state, instructions, and demonstration context, excluding evaluator success and timeout judgments. Local simulation pauses during remote inference. A local adapter validates, interpolates, and converts returned targets before execution.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 1. Pause and observe","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"observe"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"another-way-to-scale-demonstrations-an-agent-as-the-policy","source_sha256":"54c70320bcab9535b6a747c355964c24173af0da949bce401036559e568b65d7"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:diagram:1:0:prompt","title":"2. Build the prompt","html":"<p>The harness packs three things next to the current observation. The first is the task text, &quot;Pick up the vial and place it in the rack&quot;.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"prompt"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/agent-policy-loop.html","term":"prompt"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:diagram:1:0:ask","title":"3. Ask the agent","html":"<p>The prompt goes to a remote model service as a single request. The simulator stays paused until the answer comes back, so slow inference never turns into extra motion in the scene.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"ask"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/agent-policy-loop.html","term":"ask"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:diagram:1:0:adapt","title":"4. Check and adapt","html":"<p>Before anything moves, the local runtime parses the reply and validates it. The action adapter then interpolates the targets and converts them into simulator units.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"adapt"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/agent-policy-loop.html","term":"adapt"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:cue:12","title":"Motion quality and collection speed","html":"<p>The system observes again after up to H targets. Shorter chunks increase correction opportunities and remote calls. Demonstration context does not update model weights, and simulated motion time differs from collection wall time including response waits.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 5. Execute, then repeat","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"execute"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"another-way-to-scale-demonstrations-an-agent-as-the-policy","source_sha256":"8d46f08b9aff460983343c0355f279efddb9d63a51430d920e9592a4096f4afe"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:video:1:1","title":"Inspect the agent rollout","html":"<p>Now watch the agent’s motion in the external and wrist views. Follow the approach and grasp as observation, target selection, and execution repeat. Distinguish motion time in the video from collection time that includes remote-response waits.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":1}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","pane":1,"media":1,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3:cue:15","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Whatever the collection method, we ultimately need to evaluate the learned policy. We will now test a prepared checkpoint in simulation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--09-state-machine-learning--3","kind":"slide","index":34}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"09-state-machine-learning.html","anchor":"another-way-to-scale-demonstrations-isaac-autodata","source_sha256":"619722732ba373267d1f859b8b27f47f003724bfced62dc806736d9a289afba8"}}]},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","index":34,"title":"Sim Evaluation · Execution and Comparison Conditions","steps":[{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:cue:0","title":"The evaluation setup","html":"<p>We now evaluate a checkpoint trained on collected data. Stop the previous client, start the policy server, and wait for readiness. The server performs inference; lerobot_eval runs and evaluates the environment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-policy-evaluation","source_sha256":"497826bcc24e636f73c85aee184dd03c374102d54ae04421e8b53bb046e63c1f"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:cue:19","title":"Establish a baseline","html":"<p>Record the model, task, two cameras, instruction, seed, episode count, and 16-action horizon for the first ten episodes. The baseline’s default starting pose lies outside the training distribution, so failures do not by themselves establish poor weights.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":0,"kind":"command","index":4,"blocks":"4"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-policy-evaluation","source_sha256":"53abff4cd6273dc33642937440600e32cf9f6272e2ace8925eb27401f6b257cd"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:cue:30","title":"Change the starting pose","html":"<p>Next add start_pose_dataset under otherwise matched conditions. It applies mean episode-start joint positions, including the gripper, rather than sampling a pose per trial. Check printed means and standard deviations and the applied pose, then compare approach and success counts.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"compare-a-dataset-derived-starting-pose","source_sha256":"97bf40c98a5c632baba0a06c3bbbd7df24367be3c71e218d3d78865b3ea3b0bc"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:cue:25","title":"What the demonstrations end with","html":"<p>Baseline demonstrations end at release, so the policy may not return home. Learned return motion differs from a simulator reset. Repeated operation requires demonstrations that include returning to a ready pose after placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"what-happens-after-the-release","text":"The state-machine demonstrations used for this model ended without returning home, so they did not teach the policy that","accordion":null}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"what-happens-after-the-release","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"what-happens-after-the-release","source_sha256":"0bf7edea0c6e014ff422c5e48d5acf4e75dd92fd7adb1be08718115922b8c6c1"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:cue:34","title":"Display versus policy inputs","html":"<p>Headless omits browser display while retaining cameras all and rendering. Camera renaming aligns simulation keys to checkpoint keys; it does not correct framing or calibration. To test DR, change the task and active randomization rather than merely toggling display.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"evaluate-without-a-display","source_sha256":"8c08f46213c934eb4508bb7c16a5fea3bda4f70976500abd27b262bf2f9b3295"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1:cue:39","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have configured and run the policy evaluation. Next, we will record successes and failures and finish the evaluation processes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--1","kind":"slide","index":35}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-policy-evaluation","source_sha256":"0a9847ccc9417d6e631efaaaf8ff96ace68419f72761de573ec267b74a1dc40f"}}]},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2","index":35,"title":"Sim Evaluation · Observe, Record, and Stop","steps":[{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2:cue:0","title":"What to record","html":"<p>Record success counts alongside termination reasons such as placement, timeout, or unsafe rack state. Resets are not successes. Record missed grasps, drops, and collisions, and distinguish nominal 30 Hz from throughput including inference delay.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"watch-and-record-results","source_sha256":"b2d02c82ddc403167cba8837b408cb072f1c597a1f01f9068f2f4f5102d6aab9"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2:cue:6","title":"Clean up the evaluation","html":"<p>After the evaluation client finishes, stop its background server using the recorded PID and verify termination. The container and terminal may remain for the next exercise, but the previous inference process should release GPU and port resources.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 3 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"stop-evaluation","source_sha256":"39a60927ceca74ec76d98ef4eda4b84fc3d75278f7c9ad9b44c4a9149d39197d"}},{"id":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2:cue:10","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have recorded the results and failure types from the morning exercises. We will take questions before testing the same policy on the real robot this afternoon.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--11-sim-evaluation--2","kind":"slide","index":36}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"11-sim-evaluation.html","anchor":"watch-and-record-results","source_sha256":"12b1884919e2832a8515a7246d18fdface324cfc4c8393bb216cd38ece786ca1"}}]},{"id":"qa-1235--start","index":36,"title":"Q&A","steps":[{"id":"qa-1235--start:cue:0","title":"Morning Q&A","html":"<p>This morning connected calibrated inputs, demonstrations, GR00T, and simulation evaluation. Discuss questions in terms of mismatched inputs and outputs or the conditions in which failures occurred.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"qa-1235--start","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"89d41f9acf7a2f1a1bdbcc1ed0f3ab6b5cc4f21a51f5cd996cb29b4616488c3e"}},{"id":"qa-1235--start:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>It is time for lunch. We will reconvene at 13:45 to recap the morning and continue toward the real-robot exercises.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"qa-1235--start","kind":"slide","index":37}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"305129ec9f99abfb038cf752049427d75834cb51f035ab37914bc0d078ed03ef"}}]},{"id":"lunch--start","index":37,"title":"Lunch","steps":[{"id":"lunch--start:cue:0","title":"Lunch","html":"<p>We will resume at 13:45 after lunch to evaluate the same policy on the physical robot.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"lunch--start","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"2fea8e5b6bd00b686e1a18cf2f7719da143cfa16fccf55457b2bef847193e01e"}},{"id":"lunch--start:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Let us begin the afternoon with a brief recap of the robot and camera setup and our simulation evaluation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"lunch--start","kind":"slide","index":38}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"bc0cd726c84fe86beb304a9a72b608ac4f2a372965158cb2ad136626b625369e"}}]},{"id":"return--start","index":38,"title":"Welcome back, recap & Q&A","steps":[{"id":"return--start:cue:0","title":"Afternoon comparison baseline","html":"<p>We continue from the morning’s calibration, camera settings, and simulation results. Running the same policy on the physical robot will reveal which differences produce failures.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"return--start","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"c202ff01c17748e21bee9e2773f8dfceac03354bad0b8085fd77b5b5d55fcfc7"}},{"id":"return--start:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We will now compare simulation results with real-robot behavior, beginning with the differences between the two environments.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"return--start","kind":"slide","index":39}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"c680e54ba5c18452a871487431723c6ca9e711eb687093a1e0d638365323a2bc"}}]},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","index":39,"title":"From Simulation to the Real Robot","steps":[{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1:cue:0","title":"The transfer criterion","html":"<p>Sim-to-Real aims for a policy trained in simulation to perform the task on physical hardware. High simulation success can coexist with different real camera inputs and command responses; final assessment requires real rollouts.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sim-to-real-defined","source_sha256":"cdb6309013f9c25cd76ab485eab399f13fcec77d49cf82c9a6528720dc61d45f"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1:cue:7","title":"Interpret the video","html":"<p>This is simulation evaluation played at 10× speed. Successes and failures vary with layout and approach. Compare the stage that differs under matched conditions on hardware, rather than interpreting playback speed as control speed.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show video 1","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Play","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"video","operation":"play"}},{"label":"Pause","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"video","operation":"pause"}},{"label":"Restart video","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"video","operation":"replay"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"simulation-evaluation-in-action","source_sha256":"3649390218c095f6c8aa56c848a3af3de33c9cbf94ebfa4365ef270d54ad4a02"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":1,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1:cue:10","title":"Turn failures into hypotheses","html":"<p>Separating sensing, actuation, physics, and modeling gaps helps choose the next test. Ask whether the input looks different, the same command produces different motion, or contact and geometry differ.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"the-sim-to-real-gap","text":"The sim-to-real gap is often larger than expected.","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"the-sim-to-real-gap","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"the-sim-to-real-gap","source_sha256":"48ad48752fabaeaaefe72e05ba8101e22aa88307b43237be3bbc57a1530278ce"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1:cue:13","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>The same policy encounters different inputs and motion when the environment changes. We will organize these differences into four gap categories and connect them to possible responses.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--1","kind":"slide","index":40}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sim-to-real-defined","source_sha256":"373ba98595660a6a7f44fa2350985cf4c050d9855abffb94a52aad86de6973b3"}}]},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","index":40,"title":"Sim-to-Real Gap · Causes and Strategies","steps":[{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:cue:0","title":"Match the intervention to the gap","html":"<p>Two approaches address the gap: broaden the conditions experienced by the policy or align simulation with measured reality. First narrow down where the failure arises, then select the intervention.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sources-of-the-gap","source_sha256":"ab7d0ee1c2ff54195629fc07a58cac4553bb335e68a91e712c672db9a8759bf6"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:cue:4","title":"Sensing and actuation","html":"<p>Sensing gaps include lighting, lenses, blur, noise, and camera mounting. Actuation gaps arise when backlash, friction, actuator limits, or timing make identical commands produce different motion. Image comparison and joint-response measurement provide different evidence.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Sensing: what the policy sees","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"sensing"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sources-of-the-gap","source_sha256":"feaf7535185fdf932002c248a2e4b0337c134bc578352006611da152e64646d8"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:diagram:0:0:actuation","title":"Actuation: how commands become motion","html":"<p>Friction, backlash, actuator limits and timing affect how a joint follows its command. They can change with wear.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"actuation"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/reality-gap-strategies.html","term":"actuation"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:cue:6","title":"Contact and geometry","html":"<p>Physics gaps concern slipping and responses during grasp or rack contact. Modeling gaps concern link dimensions, joint frames, mass, and inertia. Identical angles can yield different poses under different joint zeros and frames, so align calibration references first.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Physics: how objects interact","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"physics"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sources-of-the-gap","source_sha256":"39b0b1d1a0867f0f92dc3f88cb61ed3f0db7a32599c14eb7517f9416f524f26a"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:diagram:0:0:modeling","title":"Modeling: how the scene is built","html":"<p>Link dimensions, joint frames and camera mounts determine the geometry of the simulated setup.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"modeling"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/reality-gap-strategies.html","term":"modeling"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:cue:8","title":"Choose the next experiment","html":"<p>DR and Cosmos expand variation, Co-Training adds real examples, and HG-DAgger adds corrections at failure states. SysID aligns simulated responses with measured motion. Even when combining methods, record changed conditions and compare which failures decrease.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Teach the policy to handle variation","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"overcome"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sources-of-the-gap","source_sha256":"3fdd176b4018c6f08caddbd5014fde26a74f0a7677d467140c4d238fc0099db2"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:diagram:0:0:close","title":"Reduce the measured mismatch","html":"<p>Measure a specific difference and adjust the simulation model. Camera calibration addresses camera geometry; system identification addresses physical response.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"close"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/reality-gap-strategies.html","term":"close"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2:cue:15","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We will start with the perception gap visible in camera images, comparing real and simulated geometry and appearance.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--2","kind":"slide","index":41}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"sources-of-the-gap","source_sha256":"db51ab306e0d03d64023c2daafc2fab6f85da8c9074a417189806c9f46e98277"}}]},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3","index":41,"title":"Perception Gap · Real / Sim Comparison","steps":[{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3:cue:0","title":"Separate geometry and appearance","html":"<p>Compare real and simulated images using stable features such as rack edges, holes, and the gripper. Overlays reveal position and shape differences; side-by-side views reveal brightness and texture. Precise comparison also requires matched poses and object placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"explore-the-perception-gap","source_sha256":"4a4b16c68704f86f28317849d3195b90e7819ace40ac954f40751c92bf477ce9"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3:cue:6","title":"Adjust camera response","html":"<p>Gain, gamma, saturation, and grain adjust appearance through camera-specific profiles. Wrist blur uses a separate path, sampled at reset and held until the next reset. Display, recording, and policy use the processed output, so inspect the actual images and label consistency.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 7 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"explore-the-perception-gap","source_sha256":"f1a7b545b8c73dfb38aed842afeb4f375f851d2a8227ff41a3f350122b2055c6"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3:cue:16","title":"Align inputs before comparing policies","html":"<p>Similar images can still leave contact and timing differences. Before changing models, align camera mapping, joint order and units, calibration, action representation, and starting pose. Real rollouts then help relate remaining failures to policy and data.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"what-makes-transfer-hard","text":"Before attributing a failure to the policy, check camera assignments, joint order and units, calibration, action convent","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"what-makes-transfer-hard","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"what-makes-transfer-hard","source_sha256":"c06892c333c83c21814c040a4689cc67993f3b4c696624e9f67e29ababf438e2"}},{"id":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3:cue:21","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We now have a basis for comparing camera inputs. Next, we will run the sim-only policy on the real robot and relate observed failures to these gaps.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--03-sim-to-real--3","kind":"slide","index":42}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"03-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"explore-the-perception-gap","source_sha256":"f56625505ab5403b394fd72111f5086200452139870265a4643cc6198b60bc25"}}]},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","index":42,"title":"Real Rollout · Sim-only Baseline","steps":[{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1:cue:0","title":"The first real baseline","html":"<p>The first model uses only 500 state-machine simulation demonstrations, concentrated on particular grasps and vial positions, without return-to-home examples. Record its real approach, placement, and subsequent behavior as the baseline.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"workspace-prep","source_sha256":"6b5bfcf902ac72950132b44940d14670929edcf891152f54bc34cd2010daad74"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1:cue:10","title":"Configure model and inputs","html":"<p>Stop the simulation client and server, then set the real checkpoint, instruction, follower port and ID, and both cameras. This model uses wrist and external keys. Match arm degrees, gripper percentages, and 16-action return and execution lengths, with RTC compensation disabled.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 3 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-the-first-policy-trained-on-sim-only-data","source_sha256":"67fc8c43c5995175fe13d482ca4cd2b010c5a0f408fa5232e5dd2edc5c4f1ee9"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1:cue:27","title":"Motion before pressing P","html":"<p>Before waiting for policy start, rollout moves the arm to a dataset-derived pose. Clear that path, inspect the reached pose and gripper opening, and confirm rack and pickup-area visibility. Focus the terminal and press P to begin a trial.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":4,"blocks":"4"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"what-to-expect","source_sha256":"12a1ae974aebde4cf16a96c4014c81a634170f205e1c650e8eda882a63844f96"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1:cue:29","title":"Score and reset safely","html":"<p>Use S and F to record success or failure, and N to discard a trial. Reposition objects only after return motion finishes and Paused appears. Exit with Q, Esc, or Ctrl+C and wait for cleanup; this strategy does not terminate automatically from duration alone.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Enlarge table","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","pane":2,"kind":"table","index":0}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"what-to-expect","source_sha256":"ed32fb62958db82911fd8c727efd86a27b026fa231b8f15888d5a94b919723d9"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1:cue:36","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>The first sim-only policy gives us a baseline. Next, we will compare policies trained with human simulation teleoperation and real demonstrations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--1","kind":"slide","index":43}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"workspace-prep","source_sha256":"3671b60402348f9feba0bee50bf15b78b4637f9e0f1abc94f03f543003b36227"}}]},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","index":43,"title":"Real Rollout · Comparing Data Sources","steps":[{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2:cue:0","title":"Experience beyond episode count","html":"<p>Compare checkpoints trained from different sources on the same station. The automated 500-episode and human 75-episode simulation sets differ in motion and end behavior as well as size. Their recipes differ too, so results do not isolate episode count.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-the-second-policy","source_sha256":"6d179338b00cdcfe1ccd4911bca7b37c5566ae9407cb46b525158acd1cd1db17"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2:cue:18","title":"Human sim 75","html":"<p>Human simulation demonstrations include varied approaches and returns after placement. Apply this checkpoint’s starting dataset and camera settings and inspect whether the policy itself returns to ready. Distinguish that behavior from controller homing after trial completion.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":0,"kind":"command","index":5,"blocks":"5"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-the-second-policy","source_sha256":"f3b7d2d72d3d0995fb3526c7fc5fe9767699c1cca9162d8729c18fe6601b123d"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2:cue:40","title":"Revised state machine 125","html":"<p>The next model uses 125 simulation episodes from a state machine revised to imitate human motion. The guide reports 8 successes in 20 real trials. Starting poses, grasps, and lift timing differ despite both being automated collection; relate these to failures in your own trials.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":0,"kind":"command","index":11,"blocks":"11"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-the-second-policy","source_sha256":"0b087217797b6f4e3dafa317f164b40b9202701256605eb9404b8e9495e4b292"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2:cue:62","title":"Real-only reference","html":"<p>Johnny42 is a reference trained on 50 real demonstrations. Its fixed-camera key is front rather than external, and its instruction must match. Its recipe freezes vision and language, so this is not a data-source-only experiment. Restore camera settings when switching back.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":5,"blocks":"5"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"optional-compare-johnny42-trained-on-real-data","source_sha256":"f5ca306e207392d8bdc152b022a888d2846b4104b7d8bc9b1c569fe38cab08f9"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2:cue:68","title":"A consistent comparison record","html":"<p>Record starting pose and layout, first-action delay, grasp and placement success, and autonomous return consistently across trials. Finish cleanup before switching models so no competing device-control or GPU process remains.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"key-takeaways","text":"Overfitting to particular grasps and vial positions can yield high simulation evaluation success rates that do not trans","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"key-takeaways","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"key-takeaways","source_sha256":"2b6c158830888f57712ba10c4693688e80af74a64445ab17a884d405a9ab69c3"}},{"id":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2:cue:69","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have compared behavior across data sources. Before changing a model that behaves unexpectedly, let us review which settings to check first.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--12-real-evaluation--2","kind":"slide","index":44}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"12-real-evaluation.html","anchor":"run-the-second-policy","source_sha256":"e715a53596132fc94c498369346e3cc8d7203e16514539915224ead5a55d65c5"}}]},{"id":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1","index":44,"title":"Debugging Sim-to-Real Issues","steps":[{"id":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1:cue:0","title":"Find the first mismatch","html":"<p>Diagnose the first mismatch across hardware execution, observations, and policy predictions. Check calibration, fasteners, and joint mapping; compare actual camera and model inputs; then compare starting poses and layouts with training data.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-debugging-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"debugging-sim-to-real-issues","source_sha256":"fcded3eb120f2401478a8e57479ee22e00787c6a51ebc9d871fafb24a0f5f02c"}},{"id":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1:cue:2","title":"Replay and offline inference","html":"<p>Recorded-command replay bypasses the policy to test execution; offline inference tests predictions on saved observations without moving the robot. Preserve runtime units and calibration, preprocessing and decoding. Change one condition at a time and record the result to guide the next fix.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1","pane":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"debugging-sim-to-real-issues","text":"These checks come from this workshop’s SO-101 vial-to-rack case study. They are useful hypotheses to test, but the cause","accordion":null}}],"pane":0,"anchor":"debugging-sim-to-real-issues","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"12-debugging-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"debugging-sim-to-real-issues","source_sha256":"a9ddc7517cb39da6d26459bfe80e63eee2f5485d27a46a98f5590c915e64e4c8"}},{"id":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1:cue:4","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Once the input and output setup is checked, missing examples of real conditions can be addressed through data. Next is co-training with simulation and real demonstrations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--12-debugging-sim-to-real--1","kind":"slide","index":45}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"12-debugging-sim-to-real.html","anchor":"debugging-sim-to-real-issues","source_sha256":"f7b9c6d2be927f9e06a753285ed2794e38f64115e1a3b21b910523aa529ada92"}}]},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","index":45,"title":"Co-Training · Sim and Real Data","steps":[{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:cue:0","title":"Train with simulation and reality","html":"<p>Co-Training combines varied simulation examples with real camera, contact, and actuator experience in one policy. Our comparison checkpoint uses 75 simulation teleoperation demonstrations and ten real demonstrations.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-strategy2-cotraining.html","anchor":"what-is-co-training","source_sha256":"e433e185d8448e62973fa1beda2b94592f04bc7d55fd1f4907bc6f4ccc59a40e"}},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:diagram:0:2:sources","title":"1. Review both sources","html":"<p>Simulation makes repeated collection and controlled variation easier. Real demonstrations show the cameras and robot response at a deployment station.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","pane":0,"media":2,"term":"sources"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/cotraining.html","term":"sources"}},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:cue:17","title":"Two checks for a mixture","html":"<p>First align camera keys and geometry, joint order, units, gripper convention, frame rate, and instruction. Then inspect sampling proportions. Longer episodes supply more windows, so the 75-to-10 episode ratio does not determine training exposure.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 2. Check exposure","action":{"slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","pane":0,"media":2,"kind":"diagram","term":"exposure"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-strategy2-cotraining.html","anchor":"what-is-co-training","source_sha256":"b8819c76df3aad510b587ea9376b25d55e033359ffb089adbdcf778cd2aef5cc"}},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:diagram:0:2:policy","title":"3. Train one policy","html":"<p>One GR00T 1.7 policy learns from both sources. Match joint order, units, action convention, gripper calibration, camera geometry and names, frame rate, and instruction.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","pane":0,"media":2,"term":"policy"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/cotraining.html","term":"policy"}},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:cue:19","title":"What to compare with the baseline","html":"<p>Ask which grasp or placement failures decrease after adding real examples. Keep simulation and real results separate and evaluate under baseline conditions. If gains are limited, inspect missing real situations rather than assuming a universally correct mixture ratio.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 4. Evaluate separately","action":{"slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","pane":0,"media":2,"kind":"diagram","term":"evaluate"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-strategy2-cotraining.html","anchor":"what-is-co-training","source_sha256":"1ee8c565198f23c1fd05e2c102229929c96dc8dd462ced9e8f2500491cbe14bc"}},{"id":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1:cue:22","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have established why the two sources are combined. Next, we will examine co-training results and the real demonstrations that were added.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--13-strategy2-cotraining--1","kind":"slide","index":46}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"13-strategy2-cotraining.html","anchor":"what-is-co-training","source_sha256":"82898aa1ebf1a78855dfb38a90ee9577a91b36a056f5c0f559c37b60fb04677b"}}]},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","index":46,"title":"Co-Training · Results and Demonstrations","steps":[{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1:cue:0","title":"Provided results and our evaluation","html":"<p>In the prepared Co-Training videos, focus on placement failures where the vial tip misses a rack slot after grasping. Small-scale appearance comparisons describe those test conditions; record trials to establish behavior at our station.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"example-results","source_sha256":"e85f2a7f63f1d9075e3235fceb1b95f3715ac560a808d86916f8db600cd8be22"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":0,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1:video:0:1","title":"Co-training with another robot appearance","html":"<p>This evaluation uses the orange SO-101. Alongside the annotated attempt outcomes, watch whether the vial tip enters the rack after grasping. Do not attribute differences from the previous video to appearance alone; match starting conditions and scoring in our evaluation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":0,"media":1}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":0,"media":1,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1:cue:14","title":"Add your own real data","html":"<p>To repeat with your own data, record teleoperation into a separate real dataset. The reference collection of five new episodes differs from the provided model’s Real10. The real recorder uses Right to end a segment, Left to rerecord, and Esc to end the session; wait for encoding and saving.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":1,"blocks":"1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"optional-collecting-real-demonstrations-with-lerobot","source_sha256":"e8d2b3f57cc6820a334ee441bb268ee8e2c2d79f32238c48d54c274f39fc71d6"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1:cue:19","title":"Inspect both sources after merging","html":"<p>Merge completed simulation and real datasets into a new output. This combines episodes without enforcing 50/50 sampling. Real episodes follow simulation episodes; inspect at least one from each source and connect their revisions and the new dataset ID to the training configuration.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 5 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":3,"blocks":"3"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"optional-collecting-real-demonstrations-with-lerobot","source_sha256":"ddfaeda955bd80550236d92db5b7c976955e47586b315071bd389d05dbdc4057"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1:cue:26","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have inspected the added real data and the prepared results. Now we will run the co-trained policy in the same workspace and compare it with the baseline.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--1","kind":"slide","index":47}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"example-results","source_sha256":"d3d562cdb913f9c0d720f5d4217eb9a7e678ea1c6dfd3f9d6e57eedbd69baf4d"}}]},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","index":47,"title":"Co-Trained Policy · Execution and Comparison","steps":[{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2:cue:0","title":"The Co-Trained checkpoint","html":"<p>The Sim75 + Real10 model uses 20,000 updates and a 16-action window, with vision trainable and language frozen. Effective batch 64 comes from four accumulations of microbatch 16. Distinguish the guide’s 10/20 real result from your station’s results.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"workspace-prep","source_sha256":"2c263b1fde16378ab1617457242c343ca6f89dbb49dc216f654ba7a1f1212523"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2:cue:23","title":"Use the baseline procedure","html":"<p>Keep the workspace and camera mapping fixed and apply this model’s starting dataset. Check completed startup motion and framing before P, then record grasp, placement, and recovery. Reset only after Paused appears and finish cleanup on exit.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":4,"blocks":"4"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"what-to-expect","source_sha256":"a738af2057b117bbf2a36c1483785721f793d8708515b916555b7a229fa77e33"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2:cue:45","title":"Compare continued training","html":"<p>The next model adds 5,000 updates on 125 broader-layout simulation episodes after 20,000 Co-Training updates. This differs from jointly training all 210 episodes from the start; its recorded real result is 9/20. Apply the second stage’s starting data and compare layout coverage and remaining failures.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":10,"blocks":"10"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"what-to-expect","source_sha256":"d9b05dfc7fab13d20c249ff16e57d1d267b304b1b544a4290fb390e93260c314"}},{"id":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2:cue:52","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Placement failures can remain after co-training. Next, HG-DAgger lets a person demonstrate the needed action at those difficult states.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--13-cotraining-practice--2","kind":"slide","index":48}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"13-cotraining-practice.html","anchor":"hands-on-deploy-a-co-trained-policy","source_sha256":"917486c80f9fece40489e4a38dcfc888f27ff88f41e20fccd40e4dc7f79bebe1"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","index":48,"title":"HG-DAgger · Correcting Failures","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:cue:0","title":"The action needed at a failure state","html":"<p>Placement can still fail after Co-Training. HG-DAgger records the human action needed at states the policy actually reaches. Instead of only repeating successful paths, it adds examples of recovering from failure.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"what-is-hg-dagger","source_sha256":"383773c6230113f1c51dab53877a901b5d4e1fe78198c9c476ecb2948caddbda"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:cue:7","title":"Collection and training are separate","html":"<p>A correction is a supervised action target for the current state. Collection does not update weights immediately or run online reward-based learning. Inspect corrections, train offline with the original data, and then evaluate the new checkpoint.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 2 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"what-is-hg-dagger","source_sha256":"8075551e8c903cb926ee45102ffaff59dc751fc0ba1111154b7961b3046cc9a0"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:0:0:attempt","title":"1. Watch the policy","html":"<p>A policy can reach states that successful demonstrations rarely show: a missed grasp, an awkward approach, or a partial lift. HG-DAgger collects a useful human action at those policy-visited states.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"attempt"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger.html","term":"attempt"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:cue:11","title":"Transfer control safely","html":"<p>Pause the policy, align the leader to the follower, and wait for correction_ready. Clear the path because the physical leader moves under motor power. Support the arm before releasing torque, perform the correction, save it, and return control to the policy.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · 2. Pause and align","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"align"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"what-is-hg-dagger","source_sha256":"eeb228a8d5b3c2203b5d9e0efc406a3108617d757c13d77cdf63326982140b35"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:0:0:correct","title":"3. Record a correction","html":"<p>Guide a useful grasp, lift, or placement and save the correction. The workshop records saved human correction segments, not the preceding autonomous attempt, pause, or alignment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"correct"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger.html","term":"correct"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:0:0:offline","title":"4. Post-train offline","html":"<p>Review corrections, combine them with original demonstrations, and run a separate post-training job. Keeping examples of motions that already work helps test whether the updated policy retains those skills.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"offline"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger.html","term":"offline"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:0:0:autonomous","title":"5. Reevaluate unaided","html":"<p>Evaluate the updated policy without human help. Assisted collection successes are not autonomous evaluation successes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":0,"media":0,"term":"autonomous"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger.html","term":"autonomous"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:cue:27","title":"Control status and evaluation meaning","html":"<p>In the recording UI, green denotes policy control and red human correction. Only human segments are saved, excluding autonomous attempts, pauses, and alignment. Assisted collection success is not autonomous success; verify improvement in intervention-free trials after training.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"how-it-works","text":"Watch the policy attempt the task. Look for a repeatable failure, such as grasping without lifting.","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"how-it-works","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"how-it-works","source_sha256":"e1812639090ccb2ba1621298356b3a15db2e904b69323048017ad394a47a1b97"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":2,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:video:2:1","title":"Pause and correction segment","html":"<p>Watch this shorter excerpt and distinguish the autonomous attempt, pause, and human correction. Identify which segment is retained as training data. Record assisted completion separately from autonomous success.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":2,"media":1}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":2,"media":1,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:1:0:demonstrations","title":"Demonstrations","html":"<p>A demonstration pairs what the robot observes with the human action taken next. Several successful attempts cover a band of familiar states, rather than every situation the robot might encounter.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"demonstrations"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger-state-map.html","term":"demonstrations"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:1:0:drift","title":"Unfamiliar states","html":"<p>A small action error changes the next observation. For example, the vial tip may end up between rack slots—a situation that successful demonstrations rarely show.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"drift"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger-state-map.html","term":"drift"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:1:0:takeover","title":"Human takeover","html":"<p>You choose when the policy needs help. Pause, align the leader with the follower, then take control.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"takeover"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger-state-map.html","term":"takeover"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:1:0:correction","title":"Human correction","html":"<p>Demonstrate the recovery with the leader arm: for example, lift the vial, move its tip over an open slot, and lower it into place. Save the useful human-controlled segment.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"correction"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger-state-map.html","term":"correction"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:1:0:handback","title":"Hand control back","html":"<p>Guide the robot out of the failure, save the useful correction, then let the policy resume. The green segment is human-controlled recovery; it is not an autonomous policy success.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"handback"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger-state-map.html","term":"handback"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:diagram:1:0:data","title":"Recovery examples","html":"<p>Corrections use the normal recording process that pairs observations and joint positions. Repeat at different failures, ideally of the same failure mode.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","pane":1,"media":0,"term":"data"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/hg-dagger-state-map.html","term":"data"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1:cue:31","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have followed the correction-collection process. Next, we will inspect which segments enter the dataset and how these short examples are used in training and evaluation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--1","kind":"slide","index":49}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"what-is-hg-dagger","source_sha256":"4c224eb39c7062a05f5ff94332302b1d8923f896a0b92343ab54f34a86556ed5"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","index":49,"title":"Corrections · What to Save and Compare","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2:cue:0","title":"The unit of correction data","html":"<p>The provided dataset contains 29 human correction segments, about 118 seconds and 3,544 frames. These are not 29 complete tasks: each runs from a difficult state to saving, with two cameras, state, and actions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"example-dataset-just-the-corrections","source_sha256":"67b39a223751bbce5c6f8d8702956222b895d73b1b909bc2f67b60b26a1220aa"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2:cue:6","title":"When to intervene","html":"<p>Pause before continued pushing when the vial tip lies between slots, outside the rack, or contacts without entering. Use motion in both views to assess alignment, then demonstrate recovery such as lifting, realigning, and lowering.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"when-to-take-over","text":"Look at the vial tip, not just the gripper. These external-camera frames show the start of two saved corrections:","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"when-to-take-over","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"when-to-take-over","source_sha256":"17331785542c10f24d6cf5733b9eec308da49c951fb113cb60d34a76b41b563a"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2:cue:9","title":"Why short segments teach","html":"<p>The trainer samples observation/action windows, so short corrections still provide useful targets. Preserve episode boundaries so unrelated corrections are not joined. After training, compare placement improvement and retention of existing grasp and lift behavior.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","pane":1,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"when-to-take-over","text":"GR00T trains on observation/action windows sampled from episodes. An observation supplies the camera images, robot state","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"when-to-take-over","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"when-to-take-over","source_sha256":"fb9fb9e98744216b6ffd8b83efa7d9898201f957df451faae51a183d119134d3"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2:video:2:0","title":"Compare baseline and correction-trained behavior","html":"<p>Compare the baseline and HG-DAgger policies side by side in the external view. Watch slot alignment and whether grasp and lift are retained. Verify these observations with autonomous trials under matched conditions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","pane":2,"media":0}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","pane":2,"media":0,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2:cue:17","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have seen what useful corrections look like. Now we will prepare the rest poses and control setup to collect them.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger--2","kind":"slide","index":50}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-hg-dagger.html","anchor":"example-dataset-just-the-corrections","source_sha256":"d71cbc9d04f8d60999c85bf6fe02fbd6d627d8e9b6277a632ae1bc29a5313b01"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","index":50,"title":"HG-DAgger Preparation · Rest Pose and Collection","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1:cue:0","title":"The powered leader moves","html":"<p>In HG-DAgger, the physical leader moves under power at startup, alignment, reset, and normal exit, even with a simulated follower. Clear hands and laptops from the path and support the arm before releasing torque.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"prepare-the-workshop-container","source_sha256":"03b5565bb8af3be0bff1d940025e21d9316303ee7987fec6b9e9daea42d43239"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1:cue:17","title":"Save rest poses","html":"<p>Support each arm in its safe rest pose and save the current position. The command records a normal-exit reference; it does not move the arm or release torque. Without gravity compensation, the arm may fall when G or L releases torque.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"teach-the-rest-poses","source_sha256":"e495136f47a1f1b4a9e59c39cf7dfcd3438ed5a77a13eb200e3356a8632b8e6d"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1:cue:24","title":"Run real collection","html":"<p>Specify the Sim75 + Real10 policy, verified cameras and ports/IDs, and correction dataset path. The real lerobot-rollout-dagger path loads the model directly. It uses the mean start pose from 75 simulation teleoperation episodes and opens the gripper first during startup/reset, so remove any held vial.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 2 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"start-collection","source_sha256":"45e8b7fd93c88c5b81510b13c0df9640cdac1f9fe36d7977106f73fef7e9bd4f"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1:cue:30","title":"How simulation collection differs","html":"<p>Simulation uses a dedicated policy server and the current command starts from the default task pose, unlike the real path. Control it from one browser tab, store sim and real corrections separately, and clean up both client and dedicated server afterward.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 2 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":2,"kind":"command","index":2,"blocks":"2"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","pane":2,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"start-collection","source_sha256":"f1f354338d25ae993e672d8cbaca4f66b5bf2bb60ec298ea963116b903648ad3"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1:cue:34","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>The rest poses and collection setup are ready. Next, we will go through policy pause, leader alignment, correction recording, and handback in order.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--1","kind":"slide","index":51}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"prepare-the-workshop-container","source_sha256":"6e0c4a1fcd2c44e446d97a7574977205dbdb19420bf44ec8310a77df0e1d65e2"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2","index":51,"title":"HG-DAgger · Align, Correct, and Return Control","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2:cue:0","title":"Take over through verified states","html":"<p>Space runs the policy; Space again pauses it at a failure. Tab aligns the leader; wait for correction_ready. Support the leader and press G to release torque and start correction recording.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"align-correct-and-hand-back","source_sha256":"7be164381b994ab7ef446e76cf9d77417f5d382d0eb97be7789c3889dc6bfda3"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2:cue:4","title":"Save and return control","html":"<p>After correction, Space holds, saves, and returns control; confirm Episode saved. X holds an unfinished correction and G resumes it. C discards; R discards and resets. Real collection has no automatic success/timeout reset, so manage objects and results while paused.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Enlarge table","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2","pane":0,"kind":"table","index":0}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"align-correct-and-hand-back","source_sha256":"4d1a7b404b891b415822f3a2d67a715b397bfbe007cf1f4c9a170e5cc56b0909"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2:cue:9","title":"Normal exit and torque release","html":"<p>Normal exit includes movement to the rest pose; wait for completion. L releases torque and exits without moving, so support the leader first. Leader-follows does not remove the pause, Tab alignment, and readiness checks before takeover.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"review-and-finish","text":"Save a useful correction with Space , or discard it with C , before exiting. Normal exit returns the leader to its taugh","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"review-and-finish","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"review-and-finish","source_sha256":"de91b11ce19d6812e5e5942509f6c3b457cc490e952d94896fc13e83ea24900b"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2:cue:13","title":"What enters the dataset","html":"<p>Each saved human correction is one episode. Policy rollout, pauses, alignment, and resets are excluded. Inspect the segments in the Visualizer and use distinct dataset IDs for new rounds.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Enlarge table","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2","pane":2,"kind":"table","index":0}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"what-gets-recorded","source_sha256":"7ea834972b02fbb60d7c206c3b1922ea94d02feef366603a7e523a8d8a94e214"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2:cue:16","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Saved corrections are inspected and used in a separate training run. Next, we will evaluate the prepared correction-trained checkpoint without human intervention.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--2","kind":"slide","index":52}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"align-correct-and-hand-back","source_sha256":"66b8568eb0a1b962a3b24648fd8c5e87c950b923f070f794019b04c72a8e5618"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3","index":52,"title":"Autonomous Evaluation After Correction Training","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3:cue:0","title":"Where this checkpoint comes from","html":"<p>Now evaluate the provided correction-trained model. It was trained from the GR00T base on Sim75, Real10, and 29 corrections; it does not immediately include the corrections just collected. Its 114 sequences differ in length and purpose.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"evaluate-the-updated-policy","source_sha256":"58aa3ee416289cce0c44a2559977619f11b3e87dcb4ffb077c0df7524a55348b"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3:cue:9","title":"Starting conditions and results","html":"<p>The recorded real result is 14/20. Because corrections start mid-task, exclude those starts from the task-start average and use simulation demonstrations instead. Verify the reached pose and compare under baseline conditions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"id1","text":"This model was trained on 75 simulated teleoperation demonstrations, 10 real demonstrations, and 29 human correction seg","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"id1","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"id1","source_sha256":"9c4b248db0aacb1a4d50780c46d4347713076c7b0ed7bdad30272f339b477f51"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3:cue:19","title":"Evaluate without intervention","html":"<p>Do not intervene in these trials. Use the same scoring procedure and record grasp, lift, and return as well as placement. Assess both reduction in targeted failures and retention of prior behavior to evaluate correction training.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View Commands / Output · 1 line ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3","pane":1,"kind":"command","index":4,"blocks":"4"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"id1","source_sha256":"8611b01c56f038a306935efb68af3a753656562d6a7e1c458bb83331bdecf5f5"}},{"id":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3:cue:28","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have covered finding failures, adding corrections, and reevaluating autonomously. After a short break, we will continue with Cosmos and system identification.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-hg-dagger-practice--3","kind":"slide","index":53}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-hg-dagger-practice.html","anchor":"evaluate-the-updated-policy","source_sha256":"2a85ad1bd8d80c0c05a5275ceb22518ac239750f69b3e27534804d6b3c8d5c2f"}}]},{"id":"qa-1540--start","index":53,"title":"Break","steps":[{"id":"qa-1540--start:cue:0","title":"Break","html":"<p>We will take a short break before examining Cosmos for broader video conditions and SysID for aligning simulated and measured motion.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"qa-1540--start","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"46c532d95e109511b7a7ebe101d109d5e1478e4d5cfe609bb84c7915ac471420"}},{"id":"qa-1540--start:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Now we will examine Cosmos augmentation, which adds visual variation to demonstration videos.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"qa-1540--start","kind":"slide","index":54}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"seoul-workshop-schedule.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"5003bbe20c91d2735555a8ed5c1cd4ed1a715dc13cd717433126213df5eb6ce4"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","index":54,"title":"Cosmos · Controlled Video Augmentation","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:cue:0","title":"From scene variation to video augmentation","html":"<p>DR changed simulator conditions during collection. Cosmos augmentation changes the appearance of recorded video to broaden visual experience. Here Cosmos generates video, while GR00T 1.7 remains the action-prediction policy.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"beyond-domain-randomization-and-co-training","source_sha256":"a6e5c1f478d0fbc445ec303caa14308ad34d44e38f8dd016dfe402d166e8a686"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:cue:12","title":"Inputs that guide generation","html":"<p>Provide the source clip, a prompt describing the desired change, and structural controls such as edges, depth, or segmentation. These guide scene structure without guaranteeing preserved object shape or contact timing. Generation runs outside the robot’s real-time control loop.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"how-cosmos-works","text":"Consider the video augmentation workflow below. Cosmos 3 uses a prompt and controls such as edges, blur, depth, or segme","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"how-cosmos-works","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"how-cosmos-works","source_sha256":"c0cc9c40705a961cfc54b4af6bd5910064b2c75df7bffe8742469c51e704b258"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:diagram:2:0:source","title":"Start with a demonstration","html":"<p>The source clip shows the robot, objects and motion whose visual appearance you want to vary.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"source"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/cosmos-video-transfer.html","term":"source"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:diagram:2:0:prompt","title":"Describe an appearance change","html":"<p>The prompt requests a variation. It does not guarantee that only the requested property will change.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"prompt"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/cosmos-video-transfer.html","term":"prompt"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:diagram:2:0:control","title":"Guide the scene structure","html":"<p>Control signals such as edges, depth or segmentation help guide generation. They do not guarantee identical geometry or contact timing.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"control"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/cosmos-video-transfer.html","term":"control"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:diagram:2:0:generate","title":"Generate a candidate clip","html":"<p>This application of Cosmos 3 produces a visual variation for review. GR00T 1.7 remains the policy that predicts robot actions in this workshop.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":2,"media":0,"term":"generate"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/cosmos-video-transfer.html","term":"generate"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:cue:18","title":"Can the original actions still apply?","html":"<p>Reusing original states, actions, and timestamps requires generated video to depict the same motion. Exclude clips with altered rack-hole, vial, or gripper relationships or changed grasp timing. Broader appearance still requires separate training and evaluation to establish policy improvement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Check the original labels still fit","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"diagram","term":"review"}}],"pane":2,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"how-cosmos-works","source_sha256":"0053725d00cfe0b595ca248ec2bbe5601e8ef09b2e6ab37f04cfbb6eb16ae43d"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:cue:24","title":"Compare structural controls","html":"<p>Compare the source with edge-only, edge-plus-segmentation, and segmentation-only results. Judge preserved task details and motion rather than the amount of background change. The separate SO-101 example and next dataset use different recipes, so interpret each with its own settings.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 1","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":3,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"how-control-signals-shape-the-result","source_sha256":"90eadff836300465ed2ebd83775b62a43bbfe976e09d585a3bde3bde9b6b2723"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1:cue:27","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Having seen the generation process, we will inspect the results. The key question is whether the original actions and timestamps still match the generated video.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--1","kind":"slide","index":55}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"beyond-domain-randomization-and-co-training","source_sha256":"257a0f03176cbdd90a098c7152aae8461a7f6740d585e304bf37c67e7021568d"}}]},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","index":55,"title":"Cosmos Dataset · Reviewing Generated Results","steps":[{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2:cue:0","title":"The generated dataset","html":"<p>This dataset replaces both camera videos for ten of 250 simulation episodes with generated footage while retaining state, actions, and timestamps. Inspect image quality and alignment with the original actions together.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"example-dataset-cosmos-augmented-demonstrations","source_sha256":"98837e5cc987e2868a1f8e5fac71dde55a30f0f1978b62b54a759899db58fb4e"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2:cue:6","title":"Keep or reject examples","html":"<p>Inspect episodes over time for consistent vial caps, rack colors, gripper contact, and object geometry. Color and geometry drift are recorded in this example, and policy improvement has not been tested. Exclude changes that the original actions cannot explain.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 1","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"example-dataset-cosmos-augmented-demonstrations","source_sha256":"c6a72f4d2ccfeddbb37663dfd46028481d508a8667a439c3a0b708fc777da64e"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2:cue:12","title":"Test augmentation benefit","html":"<p>Select clips with useful visual variation and consistent labels. Evaluate policies with and without augmentation under matched conditions to determine its benefit. Today we inspect prepared results rather than generating footage at each station.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"discussion-assess-video-augmentation","text":"Use the source and generated examples above to discuss:","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"discussion-assess-video-augmentation","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"discussion-assess-video-augmentation","source_sha256":"ed1d2d1f751dfa61383c8d02a93a23c8ea685073480e56632f04d5ce81265307"}},{"id":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2:cue:17","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>So far, we have worked on the conditions and content of the data. Next, system identification uses measured real motion to adjust simulated dynamics.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--14-strategy3-cosmos--2","kind":"slide","index":56}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"14-strategy3-cosmos.html","anchor":"example-dataset-cosmos-augmented-demonstrations","source_sha256":"21a00bec5ae61915b07fca5c97c56ed06b797bd25fc57c5f2bfc7cc8767cf419"}}]},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","index":56,"title":"SysID · Matching Simulation Through Measurement","steps":[{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:cue:0","title":"From data coverage to physical response","html":"<p>So far we have expanded the policy’s data. SysID estimates simulation parameters from measured commands and motion. The same position target can yield different tracking and settling under different stiffness, damping, effort limits, armature, and friction.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"from-measurements-to-a-simulation-model","source_sha256":"4d9968e1d32a9d90ede6e08b1528a1893fe330adf773b59f70c0f0498e1806ab"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:diagram:0:1:excite","title":"Use informative motions","html":"<p>Moving through a range of speeds reveals response differences that a single held pose cannot.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"excite"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/system-identification.html","term":"excite"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:cue:8","title":"Measurement and fitting","html":"<p>Align command and readback timing, units, calibration, and initial conditions. Fit parameters on some motions, then validate lag, amplitude, and settling on held-out motions. Do not attribute all readback delay to actuator mismatch.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Diagram · Record what the robot does","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":0,"media":1,"kind":"diagram","term":"measure"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"from-measurements-to-a-simulation-model","source_sha256":"610d8d22feafb9308beb32c5debfa5ab27f04e16245b513891c46bf4d7317d80"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:diagram:0:1:fit","title":"Fit simulation parameters","html":"<p>Adjust parameters that affect response, such as stiffness, damping and friction, to reduce disagreement with the measurements used for fitting.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"fit"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/system-identification.html","term":"fit"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:diagram:0:1:validate","title":"Test motions excluded from fitting","html":"<p>A model should explain more than the motions used to tune it. Compare response on held-out recordings before drawing conclusions about the fit.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"validate"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/system-identification.html","term":"validate"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:diagram:0:1:policy","title":"Evaluate the full manipulation task","html":"<p>Closer actuator tracking does not establish a better policy success rate. Pick-and-place adds sensing, contact and learned behavior to the test.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show on diagram","action":{"kind":"diagram","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":0,"media":1,"term":"policy"}}],"narration":{"mode":"diagram-button","source":"assets/interactive/system-identification.html","term":"policy"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:cue:14","title":"The artifact compared here","html":"<p>Anchor-Lab supplies timestamped hardware measurements; here we examine comparisons using AnchorBench’s fitted USD layer. We are not fitting a new model in this exercise. Validate improved tracking and full pick-and-place success separately.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"anchor-lab-and-anchorbench","text":"NVIDIA Anchor-Lab is a collection of timestamped measurements from physical robot experiments. It covers SO-101 arms and","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"anchor-lab-and-anchorbench","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"anchor-lab-and-anchorbench","source_sha256":"1db53c2fd8ebf9051f1ea03691ae5b4261a348e53cf6998dba1d7b557ae37e78"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1:cue:16","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have distinguished measurement, fitting, and validation. Now we will compare the default and fitted simulations with the measured hardware response.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--1","kind":"slide","index":57}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"from-measurements-to-a-simulation-model","source_sha256":"7ff804e09ebf27cd0caff84447167b47df6fff61184b517a5f6f67291137471a"}}]},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","index":57,"title":"SysID · Default, Fitted, Measured","steps":[{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2:cue:0","title":"Compare three responses","html":"<p>Yellow is default simulation, red is the fitted layer, and light blue is hardware readback. Compare lag, amplitude, and settling under the same command. Readback latency itself also affects timing.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"teleop-comparison-default-and-sysid-assets","source_sha256":"23f7f1749df8fc68e04ca7b1be473f1ff4b1434a322085d6d0e5c0362a08f24b"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":0,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2:video:1:0","title":"Watch the chirp sweep","html":"<p>This clip shows a chirp sweep with gradually increasing command frequency. Watch how the arm tracks the motion as it becomes faster. Then inspect delay and amplitude differences in the response plot.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show and play video","action":{"kind":"show","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":1,"media":0}}],"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":1,"media":0,"operation":"replay"},"narration":{"mode":"video-demo"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2:cue:10","title":"What the chirp reveals","html":"<p>A chirp gradually increases command frequency, exposing response differences hidden at a static pose. Inspect tracking lag and amplitude at higher frequencies and verify that the fitted model also explains held-out motion.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show image 2","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":1,"media":1,"kind":"show"}},{"label":"Enlarge image","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":1,"media":1,"kind":"zoom","image":0}},{"label":"Close enlarged view","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","pane":1,"media":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"example-sysid-motion-chirp-sweep","source_sha256":"3f5dbf28908d46448357cc26b79d15fab8d27a8b748f1321f98c6704687e0ec4"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2:cue:11","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>We have examined how well the motion response matches. Next, we will look at the effect of the actuator profiles on the complete pick-and-place task.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--2","kind":"slide","index":58}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"teleop-comparison-default-and-sysid-assets","source_sha256":"16aa4614b1cec28d0170ab50dbf71bb26a8eaad632f6a8edf99661df7a537f2f"}}]},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","index":58,"title":"SysID · Comparing Performance Under Matched Conditions","steps":[{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3:cue:0","title":"Connect response to task performance","html":"<p>After matching motion response, compare task outcomes separately. Paired video shows approach and contact differences for corresponding trials. Distinguish reaching a graspable pose from completing placement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"workshop-comparison-sysid-and-original-actuators","source_sha256":"1bb253716903c38f17fb7c8113bad6a8c495d354335fa78cc543c7fa461dfceb"},"media_cue":{"kind":"video","slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","pane":0,"media":0,"operation":"replay"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3:cue:6","title":"Read the two table axes","html":"<p>Rows indicate the actuator profile used to generate training data; columns indicate the evaluation profile. Each cell contains 100 simulation trials with matched resets, a 16-action horizon, and a 30-second limit. Separate training-data effects from evaluation-profile changes for a fixed policy.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Enlarge table","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","pane":1,"kind":"table","index":0}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"simulation-evaluation-results","source_sha256":"4ea3448f5a1bd2780c09be367563dd87c38aa3f6e66077aa440cfe2e5eb38116"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3:cue:7","title":"What these results establish","html":"<p>The SysID-data policy succeeded 96 and 86 times across the two profiles; the original-data policy succeeded 57 and 49 times. Profile changes for fixed policies yield differences of ten and eight percentage points. These simulation results require real trials before making claims about physical improvement.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 2 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","pane":1,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","pane":1,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":1,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"simulation-evaluation-results","source_sha256":"4ea3448f5a1bd2780c09be367563dd87c38aa3f6e66077aa440cfe2e5eb38116"}},{"id":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3:cue:12","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>SysID requires both response comparisons and task evaluation. Now we will choose one of the methods we have covered and define an experiment to try.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--15-strategy4-sysid--3","kind":"slide","index":59}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"15-strategy4-sysid.html","anchor":"workshop-comparison-sysid-and-original-actuators","source_sha256":"36ae6628f28cc7e71bb2a3812d97511dca12ebfb2fb885c79e547c8700fbb4b3"}}]},{"id":"topic--15-explore--1","index":59,"title":"Explore · Change One Factor at a Time","steps":[{"id":"topic--15-explore--1:cue:0","title":"Define the next question","html":"<p>Choose one recurring failure and define the variable to change, conditions to hold fixed, and outcome to measure. For example, fix checkpoint and starting pose while varying lighting to make the result interpretable.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"choose-an-experiment","source_sha256":"77a7203bd0860f041c039056d456a98164f74569b3e97e064e6989ecc229679b"}},{"id":"topic--15-explore--1:cue:5","title":"Test a scene change","html":"<p>Add scene variations individually on a new branch. Preserve rack access and arm clearance, inspect both views across resets, and evaluate the same policy. Without retraining on the changed conditions, this tests the existing policy’s robustness.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--1","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"try-it-change-the-scene","text":"What might change in the robot’s real workspace? Use the Architecture Tour and Domain Randomization to choose one variat","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"try-it-change-the-scene","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"try-it-change-the-scene","source_sha256":"7f3f6d9905e4c46e986bbb8b8c1a2dbdcabd77d19cab8ea6f9b1fa0041b78872"}},{"id":"topic--15-explore--1:cue:8","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Beyond varying a single condition, we can expand the workspace itself. Let us look at an example of using Astra to create a laboratory scene.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--1","kind":"slide","index":60}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"choose-an-experiment","source_sha256":"5ffb44959642d7dff8e0eb7322a70d8f2b0c32247019817a5f6f9e45ce163165"}}]},{"id":"topic--15-explore--2","index":60,"title":"Astra · Exploring a New Workspace","steps":[{"id":"topic--15-explore--2:cue:0","title":"Expand the workspace","html":"<p>The Astra example expands the lightbox into a laboratory scene. Removing panels and moving the external camera to a stand changes view geometry as well as background and lighting. After building the scene, recheck observations received by the same policy.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"create-a-workspace-with-astra","source_sha256":"b4bc531fa1e555e1548b0b853204da73cac80b9a4c96953c7428d69b833604e7"}},{"id":"topic--15-explore--2:cue:3","title":"Specify what stays fixed","html":"<p>Specify both changed scene elements and preserved robot and task conditions in the prompt. Review the result on a new branch, check cameras and access, then evaluate. Agent-generated scenes and verified policy operation are separate outcomes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View command/output · 7 lines ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"command","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--2","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"create-a-workspace-with-astra","source_sha256":"8be9381368423fbadf7ab880c060e4bdd4ba6a8facf81f01739784a4227aad27"}},{"id":"topic--15-explore--2:cue:7","title":"Find evidence for follow-up questions","html":"<p>Use LeRobot, Isaac Lab, and GR00T sources for implementation questions and Anchor-Lab or AnchorBench for measurements and actuator comparisons. Retain execution environment, errors, and comparison conditions so the experiment can continue after class.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--2","pane":1,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"documentation","text":"LeRobot Documentation","accordion":null}}],"pane":1,"anchor":"documentation","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"documentation","source_sha256":"4e30f2ec0581d0b10cf05bd0c435fad1cff20e19d4481853e1713472f072aa6c"}},{"id":"topic--15-explore--2:cue:10","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>The same collection, training, and evaluation workflow applies to new scenes. Let us now review today&#x27;s work and the five strategies.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--15-explore--2","kind":"slide","index":61}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"15-explore.html","anchor":"create-a-workspace-with-astra","source_sha256":"9e1d0ec8d7a86d193472759a4b72ede2ebfa9908f8ca67a2e686f6f5e02db2a7"}}]},{"id":"topic--16-conclusion--1","index":61,"title":"Today's Learning · Planning the Next Experiment","steps":[{"id":"topic--16-conclusion--1:cue:0","title":"Return to the workflow","html":"<p>Today we aligned arms and cameras, collected demonstrations, and connected them to GR00T training and evaluation. Real failures after simulation success helped us decide whether to change inputs, actions, data, or the physical model.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--16-conclusion--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"the-five-strategies-we-covered","source_sha256":"a53fb792cb4435a47e2ad01d543e3fcc7e1d247f3cd533e1d8772b3a32e63454"}},{"id":"topic--16-conclusion--1:cue:7","title":"The five strategies","html":"<p>DR broadens simulated conditions, Co-Training adds real experience, and HG-DAgger adds recovery at failure states. Cosmos generates candidate video variation; SysID aligns simulated responses with measured motion. Select methods from observed failures and evaluate under matched conditions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"View full table · 5 rows ↗","action":{"slide":"topic--16-conclusion--1","pane":0,"kind":"table","index":0,"blocks":"0"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--16-conclusion--1","pane":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":0,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"the-five-strategies-we-covered","source_sha256":"927c8945eff115141b4c272de2a46b020726e6c39849b8290d85b3aa0b807a7a"}},{"id":"topic--16-conclusion--1:cue:13","title":"Apply it to the next task","html":"<p>For the next task, choose one question, one changed condition, and measurable outcomes, then repeat the workflow. Feedback on where you got stuck and what helped will improve future exercises.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--16-conclusion--1","pane":2,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"id1","text":"Congratulations on finishing this course “Train an SO-101 Robot From Sim-to-Real With NVIDIA Isaac.”","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"id1","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"id1","source_sha256":"d63f0a40657fd6a7aa24dbb80c946eee1c07a1f7adf427d638a297464bd02106"}},{"id":"topic--16-conclusion--1:cue:14","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Let us discuss questions based on today&#x27;s results. Share a successful behavior, a recurring failure, or a condition you would like to test next.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--16-conclusion--1","kind":"slide","index":62}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"learning-path-summary","source_sha256":"b07cd2468cedc9bb7cac568ebc3f84e245746d61976ff89d3b98401fb681ff42"}}]},{"id":"closing-qa","index":62,"title":"Q&A","steps":[{"id":"closing-qa:cue:0","title":"Q&A","html":"<p>Let us discuss observed results and recurring failures, starting with the observation, the resulting action, and what actually happened.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"closing-qa","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"d675d20f8a9fa5a360a25adcff102175efb4779bac50e9a704f8d6a8bb7d49a7"}},{"id":"closing-qa:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Those observations and questions can become the starting point for your next experiment. Let&#x27;s close the course with that perspective.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"closing-qa","kind":"slide","index":63}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"cb236aa0095af1d689f537b8747f7d6164e7740501c6f062cecf4f4523ff66c1"}}]},{"id":"closing-end","index":63,"title":"The End","steps":[{"id":"closing-end:cue:0","title":"Closing","html":"<p>Thank you for participating. Use the recorded successes and failures to continue collection, inspection, training, and evaluation on your own task.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"closing-end","kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"3e40012e75f330acbb8ab8a6ab61aba90106c96bcb2a00e1870aa535ed673f77"}},{"id":"closing-end:cue:1","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>This concludes the main presentation. The remaining slides are reference material for questions about datasets and models, commands, troubleshooting, and setup.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"closing-end","kind":"slide","index":64}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"16-conclusion.html","anchor":"","source_sha256":"4e7aa8b9160ddc63a51370066644b23170f1318c0c7ae8b251bc200cda66084b"}}]},{"id":"topic--datasets-and-models--1","index":64,"title":"Datasets and Models","steps":[{"id":"topic--datasets-and-models--1:cue:0","title":"Model references on demand","html":"<p>These are reference slides after the main presentation. Distinguish the GR00T base from task-adapted SO-101 checkpoints and check data sources, revisions, camera schema, and processors. Local paths must point to the pretrained_model directory or repo root containing the policy artifacts.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--datasets-and-models--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"datasets-and-models.html","anchor":"current-model-selection","source_sha256":"165f31df68fe83747e2207e8ab12505856c83f47c2449a798d1544d24864c11c"}},{"id":"topic--datasets-and-models--1:cue:43","title":"Compatibility and evidence","html":"<p>Compatibility includes joint order and units, calibration, normalization, relative-action processing, and horizon, not just camera keys. Renaming changes names only. N1.6 artifacts and generated videos are not automatically verified N1.7 policies; distinguish reported simulation and real results.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--datasets-and-models--1","pane":3,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"checkpoint-compatibility","text":"GR00T 1.7 base model and compatible LeRobot configuration.","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"checkpoint-compatibility","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"datasets-and-models.html","anchor":"checkpoint-compatibility","source_sha256":"ab88fab52d7379376aca1320d85d1e593b812881536acc6779127a333a130675"}},{"id":"topic--datasets-and-models--1:cue:58","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>Once the model and compatibility requirements are checked, the Quick Reference provides commands for the corresponding task.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--datasets-and-models--1","kind":"slide","index":65}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"datasets-and-models.html","anchor":"datasets-and-models","source_sha256":"295fa0ca59b7cd21cd4969dd7cdf31f7ccb454be17f3de02118e481044762943"}}]},{"id":"topic--quick_reference--1","index":65,"title":"Quick Reference","steps":[{"id":"topic--quick_reference--1:cue:0","title":"Find the right command","html":"<p>Use Quick Reference to find commands for a specific task. Build, start, and attach from the host; run subsequent exercises in the container. Supply actual ports, camera indices, dataset and model paths, and finish the previous execution before starting another.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--quick_reference--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"quick_reference.html","anchor":"build-and-start-host","source_sha256":"d92e04f4f9c590d47eb0c1aa38ebc39905b8d0daa10e9d976f5e6f1776f06b10"}},{"id":"topic--quick_reference--1:cue:31","title":"Recording and evaluation essentials","html":"<p>Browser recording uses Space to start/save, C to discard, and R to discard/reset; wait for completion. Automated collection must reach its target successes and finish export. Stop both simulation client and server, and reset real objects only after Paused and completed motion.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--quick_reference--1","pane":4,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"simulation-and-recording","text":"Focus http://localhost:8090 : Space starts/saves, C discards, R discards and resets. Wait for saving to finish before ex","accordion":null}}],"pane":4,"anchor":"simulation-and-recording","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"quick_reference.html","anchor":"simulation-and-recording","source_sha256":"5dad1b03433c513fb17599b8b930d1992d05990e47f1fdb96f1f8ae3eb906bad"}},{"id":"topic--quick_reference--1:cue:64","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>The next reference slide provides symptom-specific diagnostic procedures.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--quick_reference--1","kind":"slide","index":66}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"quick_reference.html","anchor":"quick-reference","source_sha256":"b36788d7b15fa480a660b0e9faa1f3d7d499ec1659b32e3939e18240512a56c8"}}]},{"id":"topic--troubleshooting--1","index":66,"title":"Troubleshooting Guide","steps":[{"id":"topic--troubleshooting--1:cue:0","title":"Diagnose by symptom","html":"<p>Identify the failing command and whether it ran on the host or in the container, then choose the hardware, camera, policy, simulation, or dataset path. For missing motor responses, begin with power, ports, and cables; for cameras, inspect actual previews and indices.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--troubleshooting--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"troubleshooting.html","anchor":"hardware-issues","source_sha256":"19114f7a27238062cb1616fed31d821b36faa5159761aa5cec857bf717ada155"}},{"id":"topic--troubleshooting--1:cue:45","title":"Separate prediction from execution","html":"<p>Trace raw camera frames, actual model inputs, predicted actions, and executed motion to find the first mismatch. Offline inference tests prediction; replay tests execution. Replay does not automatically avoid collisions on the starting path, so prepare the device and workspace and supervise it.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--troubleshooting--1","pane":2,"media":0,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"the-arm-reaches-where-there-is-no-vial","text":"When the arm reaches where there’s no vial, find the first place the observation, prediction, and motion disagree .","accordion":null}}],"pane":2,"anchor":"the-arm-reaches-where-there-is-no-vial","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"troubleshooting.html","anchor":"the-arm-reaches-where-there-is-no-vial","source_sha256":"5efcf990a572e6651c8b2549392c76973d9972e807a6717b8dd05022d89071c9"}},{"id":"topic--troubleshooting--1:cue:66","title":"Check saved and running state","html":"<p>Check leftover servers for memory or port conflicts and recording/final-export completion for empty datasets. Browser keys need one focused tab. Capture logs and actual state before retrying, then change one condition at a time.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--troubleshooting--1","pane":4,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"dataset-and-recording-issues","text":"vials_state_machine --record stages successful episodes and assembles the LeRobot dataset after reaching --num_episodes ","accordion":null}}],"pane":4,"anchor":"dataset-and-recording-issues","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"troubleshooting.html","anchor":"dataset-and-recording-issues","source_sha256":"f5841261206cd01f18edb7389cd3568b2485a0fa4fcc3922513d73d9ca9d2277"}},{"id":"topic--troubleshooting--1:cue:72","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>The remaining reference material covers setting up a new station. First are the parts and assembly guidelines for the physical workspace.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--troubleshooting--1","kind":"slide","index":67}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"troubleshooting.html","anchor":"troubleshooting-guide","source_sha256":"9b5907eb1a7d70d10ba1727f07de285f89d192207810e2daddab14556d870404"}}]},{"id":"topic--05-building-workspace--1","index":67,"title":"Building the Workspace","steps":[{"id":"topic--05-building-workspace--1:cue:0","title":"A reproducible workspace","html":"<p>Build a new station around the reference lightbox, diffuse light, two cameras, secured robot, mat, vials, and rack. Reproducibility requires camera views that match training conditions as well as assembly dimensions.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--05-building-workspace--1","pane":0,"media":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"05-building-workspace.html","anchor":"video-tutorial","source_sha256":"fa5397ff957b6b5cb57c8b6cc950104ff61c3ae97e3ae3d155f357c5da0888f4"}},{"id":"topic--05-building-workspace--1:cue:31","title":"Check the view before mounting","html":"<p>Compare sample and live images before finalizing camera holes and mounts. Keep lighting uniform and check heat and mounting stability during long use. Clear clamps and cables from joint travel so calibration records the true range.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Open tip","action":{"slide":"topic--05-building-workspace--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"popup","id":"tip-1"}},{"label":"Close popup","action":{"slide":"topic--05-building-workspace--1","pane":3,"media":0,"kind":"close"}}],"pane":3,"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"05-building-workspace.html","anchor":"build-the-workspace","source_sha256":"5078b1ffb8634041c6ec255b64f87de63237ca9bf5ba337ac25dcfc93c3d3e84"}},{"id":"topic--05-building-workspace--1:cue:47","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>After assembling the physical workspace, we need a matching software environment. Next are the repository, container, and model preparation steps.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--05-building-workspace--1","kind":"slide","index":68}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"05-building-workspace.html","anchor":"building-the-workspace","source_sha256":"7e4ea96f39e9faa4e2bee5805cc4b78f21ed5b766456ae557d3c8b5ec2aaf792"}}]},{"id":"topic--06-get-the-code--1","index":68,"title":"Get the Code and Models","steps":[{"id":"topic--06-get-the-code--1:cue:0","title":"Prepare matching source and runtime","html":"<p>Prepare Git LFS, Docker, and the NVIDIA runtime and clone the specified branch. Build the workshop image from the repository root and mount outputs, datasets, and models. Use the repository-pinned stack rather than substituting a separate Isaac Sim installation.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--06-get-the-code--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"06-get-the-code.html","anchor":"workshop-software","source_sha256":"5e5ddfdea947112a1bdc864fc4ae38bd32ef649ab86784426d3a007668ac7819"}},{"id":"topic--06-get-the-code--1:cue:27","title":"Containers and persistent files","html":"<p>The --rm container is removed when the main shell exits, while mounted source and data remain on the host. Container-only installations and ordinary exports do not automatically persist to a new run. Attach to a running container or restart from the actual host checkout.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--06-get-the-code--1","pane":3,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"persistent-files","text":"Edits under the mounted directories persist on the host. The --rm option removes the container when its main process exi","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"persistent-files","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"06-get-the-code.html","anchor":"persistent-files","source_sha256":"4a347d346a713e81860c0ca0e0d87e56fc2facd783f602d5c3159b466112baca"}},{"id":"topic--06-get-the-code--1:cue:33","title":"Validate execution in stages","html":"<p>Validate physics-only simulation first, stop it, and then verify both cameras and ovrtx rendering. Specify camera selection and both newton/ovrtx_renderer presets, distinguishing initial cache preparation from errors in logs. Then prepare a complete compatible N1.7 policy under models.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--06-get-the-code--1","pane":4,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"check-the-simulation","text":"The first ovrtx run can take a few minutes to warm up shader caches. The interactive camera display is introduced in Tel","accordion":null}}],"pane":4,"anchor":"check-the-simulation","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"06-get-the-code.html","anchor":"check-the-simulation","source_sha256":"8e77b4ac5929af28f45c94ed5d4f45f62f30bff2c373cdc4fa82ca6a737ee917"}},{"id":"topic--06-get-the-code--1:cue:39","title":"Moving On","html":"<p>After checking the runtime, prepare the course models and starting-pose data in advance. The final reference slide brings these preparation items together.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Next slide →","action":{"slide":"topic--06-get-the-code--1","kind":"slide","index":69}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-transition","page":"06-get-the-code.html","anchor":"get-the-code-and-models","source_sha256":"39e4f70237e5e5b3f9bd92657c48263dd8536599e1f227f52d13cb51a218bdb4"}}]},{"id":"topic--workshop-preparation--1","index":69,"title":"Workshop Preparation Reference","steps":[{"id":"topic--workshop-preparation--1:cue:0","title":"Final preparation","html":"<p>Before class, finish the image build and model cache and verify that the container can read them. Download weights with configuration and processors and check precision settings for the selected revision. Restart the policy after configuration changes.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Show this slide","action":{"slide":"topic--workshop-preparation--1","pane":0,"kind":"show"}}],"narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"workshop-preparation.html","anchor":"clone-the-repository-and-build-the-container","source_sha256":"9fc84cf230b37bbef20184cd87f403bb81c9e4c6bede1dd20a096c3eb68eb0bc"}},{"id":"topic--workshop-preparation--1:cue:16","title":"Data for the starting pose","html":"<p>Prepare dataset metadata and joint-state data for starting-pose calculation. This serves a different purpose from a full download for video inspection. Because HG-DAgger corrections begin mid-task, use simulation teleoperation task-start examples for the starting pose.</p>","actions":[{"label":"Go to this explanation","action":{"slide":"topic--workshop-preparation--1","pane":3,"kind":"scroll","anchor":"download-starting-pose-data","text":"Run this from the workshop checkout on the host. It downloads metadata and joint-state data for every dataset used by --","accordion":null}}],"pane":3,"anchor":"download-starting-pose-data","narration":{"mode":"authored-essential","page":"workshop-preparation.html","anchor":"download-starting-pose-data","source_sha256":"57f2b3361907c73e0152d28402185c1bb4c7f97df488ef176dfb44cc5cc9bebb"}}]}];
